"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4286527,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Allergy Asthma Immunol Res","title":"Allergy, asthma & immunology research","essn":"2092-7363","issn":"2092-7355","nlmid":"101518382","isoabbreviation":"Allergy Asthma Immunol Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"652-667","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>Asthma is heterogeneous in its molecular and clinical manifestations, complicating treatment. In this study, we investigated the molecular phenotypes of asthma using RNA-sequencing (RNA-seq) of peripheral blood mononuclear cells to advance precision medicine approaches.<h4>Methods</h4>We analyzed peripheral blood RNA-seq data from 456 participants from the Cohort for Reality and Evolution of Adult Asthma in Korea, including 23 patients with severe asthma and 433 with non-severe asthma to identify blood transcriptome-associated clusters (B-TACs) that correlate with distinct inflammatory processes. This included a meta-analysis of differentially expressed genes to refine gene selection for defining B-TACs. Gene Set Variation Analysis was used to assess the differential activation of biological pathways across B-TACs. We validated these findings in the Precision Medicine Intervention in Severe Asthma cohort of 122 patients, focusing on cluster stability, clinical correlates, and treatment responsiveness.<h4>Results</h4>Our analysis identified 3 B-TACs (B-TAC1, B-TAC2 and B-TAC3) with B-TAC1 and B-TAC2 showing higher blood eosinophil levels than B-TAC3. B-TAC1 was characterized by a clear T2-high inflammatory profile, whereas B-TAC2 exhibited a mixed molecular phenotype with both T2-high and non-T2 features. B-TAC3 was associated with low T2 and inflammasome-related inflammation profiles. Validation using the PRISM cohort underscored the stability of B-TAC1 and B-TAC2. Notably, patients with B-TAC2 molecular phenotype had lower biologics use and tended to have less favorable treatment outcomes than those with B-TAC1.<h4>Conclusions</h4>We discovered and validated 3 B-TACs, with B-TAC2 displaying a mixed T2-high and non-T2 phenotype, suggesting a potential reduction in responsiveness to biologic treatments. These findings provide molecular insights that may guide the development of personalized treatment strategies for asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Public Health Sciences, Graduate School of Public Health, Seoul National University, Seoul, Korea.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Korea Health Industry Development Institute","acronym":"KHIDI","grantId":"RS-2024-00403700","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Health Fellowship Foundation","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Blood","Phenotype","Asthma","Biomarker","Transcriptome","eosinophils","Biologics","Precision Medicine"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4168/aair.2026.18.5.652"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13655406"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13655406?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-16","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"43016216","source":"MED","pmid":"43016216","doi":"10.53738/revmed.2026.22.983.49248","title":"[Asthma, air pollution and climate change].","authorString":"Benabdelkader L, Langhart J, Grandbastien M, Charbonnier F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Benabdelkader L","lastName":"Benabdelkader","firstName":"Linda","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Service de pneumologie, Réseau hospitalier neuchâtelois, 2000 Neuchâtel."}]}},{"fullName":"Langhart J","lastName":"Langhart","firstName":"Julia","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9923-0102"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Service de pneumologie, Hôpitaux universitaires de Genève, 1211 Genève 14."}]}},{"fullName":"Grandbastien M","lastName":"Grandbastien","firstName":"Manon","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Service de pneumologie, Centre hospitalier universitaire vaudois, 1011 Lausanne."}]}},{"fullName":"Charbonnier F","lastName":"Charbonnier","firstName":"Florian","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Service de pneumologie, Hôpitaux universitaires de Genève, 1211 Genève 14."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9923-0102"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"983","volume":"22","journalIssueId":4314527,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Rev Med Suisse","title":"Revue medicale suisse","issn":"1660-9379","nlmid":"101219148","isoabbreviation":"Rev Med Suisse"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1831-1836","abstractText":"Asthma is a chronic respiratory disease affecting more than 360 million adults and children worldwide. Outdoor air pollution as well as indoor pollution are major contributors to poor asthma control and may also play a role in disease development. Climate change further exacerbates this burden and influences asthma through additional pathways, including longer pollen seasons, thunderstorm asthma, wildfires, and flooding. Individual protective measures have shown limited effectiveness, whereas reducing exposure at the source through transportation, urban planning, and energy policies remains the most effective strategy. Clinicians have an important role to play, both in educating patients and in advocating for public health measures.","affiliation":"Service de pneumologie, Réseau hospitalier neuchâtelois, 2000 Neuchâtel.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"fre","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article","English Abstract"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"prevention & control"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Air Pollution","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Climate Change"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.53738/REVMED.2026.22.983.49248"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-12","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42948536","source":"MED","pmid":"42948536","pmcid":"PMC13711355","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13711355"]},"doi":"10.1002/hsr2.73425","title":"Integration of Aromatherapy and Essential Oils in Asthma Symptom Relief: A Narrative Review.","authorString":"Odoom A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Odoom A","lastName":"Odoom","firstName":"Alex","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5761-1564"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Microbiology University of Ghana Medical School, Korle-Bu Accra Ghana."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5761-1564"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4298707,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Health Sci Rep","title":"Health science reports","essn":"2398-8835","issn":"2398-8835","nlmid":"101728855","isoabbreviation":"Health Sci Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e73425","abstractText":"<h4>Background and aims</h4>Asthma affects 262 million people globally, causing 455,000 preventable deaths, with higher mortality in low-resource areas due to limited diagnosis and treatment. Aromatherapy, using bioactive essential oils (EOs), has emerged as a promising complementary approach for asthma symptom management.<h4>Methods</h4>This review synthesizes the current evidence on the therapeutic potential of EOs in alleviating asthma-related inflammation, bronchoconstriction, and immune dysregulation through anti-inflammatory, bronchodilatory, and immunomodulatory mechanisms.<h4>Results</h4>Key oils such as lavender, eucalyptus, and peppermint demonstrate efficacy in reducing airway hyperresponsiveness, oxidative stress, and Th2-mediated inflammation, supported by preclinical and clinical studies. Methods of application, including inhalation, topical use, and environmental diffusion, are evaluated for their physiological and psychological benefits, particularly in stress and sleep modulation, which are critical to asthma control. Safety considerations, such as allergenicity, proper dosing, and potential drug interactions, are emphasized to ensure safe integration with conventional therapies.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Despite the promising findings, further large-scale clinical trials are needed to validate the efficacy, optimize protocols, and explore personalized approaches based on genetic and phenotypic variability. This review underscores aromatherapy's role as an adjunctive therapy within a holistic asthma management framework, bridging traditional knowledge with contemporary scientific validation to improve patient outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Microbiology University of Ghana Medical School, Korle-Bu Accra Ghana.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Essential oils","Symptom","Asthma","Complementary Therapy","Aromatherapy"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/hsr2.73425"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13711355"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13711355?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1356471","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-11352104/v1","title":"Vocal acoustic parameters as biomarkers to detect asthma and its association with respiratory symptoms and comorbidities: a cross-sectional study","authorString":"de Oliveira YAP, Silva VdS, Pestana ML, Pinheiro DHA, de Lima FF, Cervera VZ, Svartman FRF, Berti LC, Martins MVM, Pinto RMdC, Agondi RC, Finger M, Carvalho CRF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"de Oliveira YAP","lastName":"de Oliveira","firstName":"Yan Anderson Pires","initials":"YAP"},{"fullName":"Silva VdS","lastName":"Silva","firstName":"Victor dos Santos","initials":"VdS"},{"fullName":"Pestana ML","lastName":"Pestana","firstName":"Mariana Lopes","initials":"ML"},{"fullName":"Pinheiro DHA","lastName":"Pinheiro","firstName":"David Halen Araújo","initials":"DHA"},{"fullName":"de Lima FF","lastName":"de Lima","firstName":"Fabiano Francisco","initials":"FF"},{"fullName":"Cervera VZ","lastName":"Cervera","firstName":"Vitoria Zacarias","initials":"VZ"},{"fullName":"Svartman FRF","lastName":"Svartman","firstName":"Flaviane Romani Fernandes","initials":"FRF"},{"fullName":"Berti LC","lastName":"Berti","firstName":"Larissa Cristina","initials":"LC"},{"fullName":"Martins MVM","lastName":"Martins","firstName":"Marcus Vinícius Moreira","initials":"MVM"},{"fullName":"Pinto RMdC","lastName":"Pinto","firstName":"Regina Maria de Carvalho","initials":"RMdC"},{"fullName":"Agondi RC","lastName":"Agondi","firstName":"Rosana Câmara","initials":"RC"},{"fullName":"Finger M","lastName":"Finger","firstName":"Marcelo","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Carvalho CRF","lastName":"Carvalho","firstName":"Celso","initials":"CRF"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>  <bold>Introduction</bold>  : Asthma is an obstructive airway disease with a high prevalence worldwide. Vocal acoustic parameters have been investigated as biomarkers in several clinical conditions; however, their use in asthma remains poorly known.  <bold>Aim</bold>  : To investigate whether vocal acoustic parameters can be used as biomarkers to detect asthma and to evaluate their association with respiratory symptoms and comorbidities.  <bold>Methods:</bold>  This cross-sectional study included 270 individuals: 180 with moderate-to-severe controlled asthma (CA) or non-controlled asthma (NCA) and 90 healthy controls (HC). Participants with asthma were classified as having CA or NCA using the Asthma Control Questionnaire (ACQ). The HC group was matched for age, sex, and body mass index (BMI). Voice samples consisted of a sustained vowel recorded on a smartphone using the SPIRA® application. Acoustic outcomes were obtained using Praat software, including fundamental frequency (F0), standard deviation fundamental frequency (SDf0), jitter, shimmer, number of voice breaks, and Harmonics-to-Noise Ratio (HNR). All analyses were stratified by sex.  <bold>Results:</bold>  No anthropometric differences were observed in either sex. Among females, both asthma groups showed higher shimmer and voice breaks and lower HNR values, but only NCA showed higher SDf0 and jitter (p<0.05). Among males, both asthma groups showed higher shimmer values, while HNR was lower only in CA compared with HC (p<0.05). No acoustic parameters differed between CA and NCA in either sex. Significant linear associations were observed between acoustic parameters, respiratory symptoms, and comorbidities in females.  <bold>Conclusion:</bold>  Vocal acoustic parameters discriminated asthma from controls but did not differentiate between asthma. These results suggest that these parameters can be used as biomarkers for detecting asthma.  </p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-11352104/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-04","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1356471","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-11-03","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42999790","source":"MED","pmid":"42999790","pmcid":"PMC13744005","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13744005"]},"doi":"10.1029/2026gh001972","title":"Asthma Exacerbations Following Wildfire Smoke PM&lt;sub&gt;2.5&lt;/sub&gt; Exposure Among U.S. Adults.","authorString":"Huang Y, Luo L, Leng S, Zhang S, Lavin RP, Buenemann M, Lin Y, Campen M, Gong X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang Y","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Yanhong","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Veterinary Physiology and Pharmacology Texas A&M University College Station TX USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biobehavioral Health The Pennsylvania State University University Park PA USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Geography & Environmental Studies University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Luo L","lastName":"Luo","firstName":"Li","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."},{"affiliation":"University of New Mexico Comprehensive Cancer Center Albuquerque NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Leng S","lastName":"Leng","firstName":"Shuguang","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."},{"affiliation":"University of New Mexico Comprehensive Cancer Center Albuquerque NM USA."},{"affiliation":"Lung Cancer Program Lovelace Biomedical Research Institute Albuquerque NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang S","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Su","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Geography & Environmental Studies University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."},{"affiliation":"Earth Data Analysis Center (EDAC) University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lavin RP","lastName":"Lavin","firstName":"Roberta P","initials":"RP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Health Equity and Preparedness College of Nursing University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Buenemann M","lastName":"Buenemann","firstName":"Michaela","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5139-739X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Geography and Environmental Studies New Mexico State University Las Cruces NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin Y","lastName":"Lin","firstName":"Yan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Geography The Pennsylvania State University University Park PA USA."},{"affiliation":"Social Science Research Institute (SSRI) The Pennsylvania State University University Park PA USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Campen M","lastName":"Campen","firstName":"Matthew","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences College of Pharmacy University of New Mexico Albuquerque NM USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gong X","lastName":"Gong","firstName":"Xi","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7899-6213"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biobehavioral Health The Pennsylvania State University University Park PA USA."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Computational and Data Sciences (ICDS) The Pennsylvania State University University Park PA USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7899-6213"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5139-739X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"10","journalIssueId":4303339,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Geohealth","title":"GeoHealth","essn":"2471-1403","issn":"2471-1403","nlmid":"101706476","isoabbreviation":"Geohealth"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e2026GH001972","abstractText":"Asthma exacerbation can be triggered by various forms of air pollution, including PM<sub>2.5</sub> from wildfire smoke. This study aims to investigate the association between wildfire smoke PM<sub>2.5</sub> exposure and asthma exacerbations in adults during the warm months (April-August) between 2006 and 2019. Using a case-crossover design, we analyzed 18,935 asthma exacerbation events and four matched control periods per event from the National Institutes of Health (NIH) <i>All of Us</i> research program. We used the conditional logistic regression to estimate the association (odds ratios [ORs]) between wildfire smoke PM<sub>2.5</sub> exposure and asthma exacerbation, considering lag periods of 0-7 days and adjusting for temperature, precipitation, and humidity. Significant positive associations were observed across all lag periods, with adjusted ORs ranging from 1.27 (95% CI: 1.22-1.33) to 1.38 (95% CI: 1.32-1.44). Stratified analyses showed higher odds of asthma exacerbation among older adults, non-Hispanic Whites, midwestern residents, and those exposed in May. These findings highlight the potential contribution of wildfire smoke PM<sub>2.5</sub> to asthma exacerbations in adults and the importance of targeted public health strategies to reduce exposure during wildfire events.","affiliation":"Department of Veterinary Physiology and Pharmacology Texas A&M University College Station TX USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD026557","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIMHD NIH HHS","acronym":"MD","grantId":"P50 MD015706","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD023205","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD026550","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD026551","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"U2C OD023196","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD025337","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD026554","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NICHD NIH HHS","acronym":"HD","grantId":"P2C HD041025","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIEHS NIH HHS","acronym":"ES","grantId":"P42 ES025589","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD025277","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIEHS NIH HHS","acronym":"ES","grantId":"R01 ES035421","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD023206","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD026548","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"R21 HL173388","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"U24 OD023121","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"U24 OD023176","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD025276","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD025315","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"OT2 OD026552","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","gran