 A","lastName":"Solakis","firstName":"Athanasios","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Private Practice, Chania, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Steiropoulos P","lastName":"Steiropoulos","firstName":"Paschalis","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Medical School, Democritus University, Alexandroupolis, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Tzanakis N","lastName":"Tzanakis","firstName":"Nikolaos","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University Hospital of Heraklion, Medical School, University of Crete, Heraklion, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Tzortzaki E","lastName":"Tzortzaki","firstName":"Eleni","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Private Practice, Irakleio, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Vittorakis S","lastName":"Vittorakis","firstName":"Stylianos","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Private Practice, Chania, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Zervas E","lastName":"Zervas","firstName":"Eleftherios","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7436-4550"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"7th Respiratory Medicine Department and Asthma Centre, \"Sotiria\" Chest Diseases Hospital, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Bakakos P","lastName":"Bakakos","firstName":"Petros","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1st University Department of Respiratory Medicine, \"Sotiria\" Chest Diseases Hospital, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Loukides S","lastName":"Loukides","firstName":"Stelios","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"2nd Respiratory Department, University General Hospital Attikon, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Samitas K","lastName":"Samitas","firstName":"Konstantinos","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"7th Respiratory Medicine Department and Asthma Centre, \"Sotiria\" Chest Diseases Hospital, Athens, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5219-6971"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7436-4550"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8736-619X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9657-3705"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0774-3942"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4456-8987"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"12","journalIssueId":4230079,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"ERJ Open Res","title":"ERJ open research","essn":"2312-0541","issn":"2312-0541","nlmid":"101671641","isoabbreviation":"ERJ Open Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1379-2025","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Severe asthma (SA) imposes a disproportionate burden on patients and healthcare systems worldwide, yet region-specific data from Southern Europe remain scarce. The Greek Severe Asthma Registry (GSAR), an initiative of the Hellenic Thoracic Society, provides the first nationwide characterisation of severe asthma in Greece.<h4>Methods</h4>We analysed data cross-sectionally from 619 adult patients (median age: 56 years; 61.1% female) with severe asthma, recruited across 13 specialised hospital centres and 11 private respiratory practices between March 2018 and November 2021. Patients were stratified by age of asthma onset, biologic therapy use and maintenance oral corticosteroid (mOCS) dependence. Clinical, inflammatory and treatment characteristics were assessed, alongside exacerbation predictors.<h4>Results</h4>The study population exhibited substantial disease burden, with 434 patients (70.1%) experiencing ≥1 exacerbation and 149 (24.1%) requiring hospitalisation in the prior year. Late-onset asthma (60.7%) was associated with a faster progression to severe disease, higher exacerbation risk (79.8% <i>versus</i> 69.5%, p=0.004), and more frequent cardiometabolic comorbidities <i>versus</i> early-onset asthma. Despite better asthma control (Asthma Control Test ≥20: 61.8% <i>versus</i> 50.5%, p=0.012), biologically treated patients (57%) had more severe disease, including lower forced expiratory volume in 1 s (FEV<sub>1</sub>) % predicted (73% <i>versus</i> 81%, p<0.001). Patients receiving mOCS (18.9%) exhibited poorer asthma control, higher exacerbation rates and more frequent corticosteroid-related comorbidities. In multivariable models, elevated blood eosinophil count (incidence rate ratio: 1.044 per 100 cells per μL) and reduced FEV<sub>1</sub>% (odds ratio: 0.97 per 1% increase) were independent predictors of exacerbations.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This study highlights the phenotypic heterogeneity of severe asthma in Greece and reveals region-specific treatment patterns and unmet clinical needs despite access to advanced therapies. These real-world findings may guide national asthma care strategies and contribute to European initiatives aimed at optimising asthma management.","affiliation":"2nd Respiratory Department, University General Hospital Attikon, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/23120541.01379-2025"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13334347"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13334347?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1365935","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-11449815/v1","title":"Cross-trait genetic analysis of asthma and eosinophils uncovers risk loci in African ancestry populations","authorString":"Sheng M, Yang Y, Wu Q, Cui L, Meng Z, Song Y, Wang X, Jiang Y, Zhang Y, Chang X, Song X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sheng M","lastName":"Sheng","firstName":"Meiling","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Yang Y","lastName":"Yang","firstName":"Yujuan","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Wu Q","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Qinfeng","initials":"Q"},{"fullName":"Cui L","lastName":"Cui","firstName":"Limei","initials":"L"},{"fullName":"Meng Z","lastName":"Meng","firstName":"Ziang","initials":"Z"},{"fullName":"Song Y","lastName":"Song","firstName":"Yingchao","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Wang X","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Xianjin","initials":"X"},{"fullName":"Jiang Y","lastName":"Jiang","firstName":"Yue","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Yu","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Chang X","lastName":"Chang","firstName":"Xiao","initials":"X"},{"fullName":"Song X","lastName":"Song","firstName":"Xicheng","initials":"X"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Asthma disproportionately affects African ancestry populations, yet its genetic architecture remains poorly characterized due to persistent underrepresentation in genomic studies. We integrated genome-wide association study (GWAS) summary statistics for asthma and white blood cell traits in African ancestry cohorts. Genome-wide and local genetic correlations were assessed using linkage disequilibrium score regression, and cross-trait associations were identified using a conditional false discovery rate framework. We identified a robust positive genetic correlation between asthma and eosinophil counts, together with several ancestry-specific local signals. Cross-trait analysis identified 78 novel pleiotropic loci, including an African-specific locus upstream of CD36, complementing evidence from East Asian populations. We also identified an African-specific association at CD226 (rs8087187), independent of previously reported European associations. Flow cytometry in 19 patients with asthma and 19 healthy controls demonstrated increased CD226 expression in CD4⁺ T cells from patients with asthma, supporting its functional relevance. Our findings reveal ancestry-specific genetic and immune mechanisms underlying asthma and highlight the importance of diverse populations in elucidating the genetic architecture of complex diseases.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-11449815/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-13","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1365935","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-11-12","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42730051","source":"MED","pmid":"42730051","pmcid":"PMC13565390","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13565390"]},"doi":"10.2147/jaa.s637738","title":"Association of Tirzepatide versus Semaglutide with Risk of Asthma Exacerbation in Patients with Asthma and Type 2 Diabetes: A US Multicenter Retrospective Cohort Study.","authorString":"Hung CT, An CY, Suk CW, Lee TW, Hung SH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hung CT","lastName":"Hung","firstName":"Chun-Tse","initials":"CT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3485-9775"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacy, College of Pharmacy, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"An CY","lastName":"An","firstName":"Chen-Yen","initials":"CY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Taipei Medical University Hospital, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Suk CW","lastName":"Suk","firstName":"Chi-Won","initials":"CW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Wan Fang Hospital, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee TW","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Ting-Wei","initials":"TW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology and Metabolism, Department of Internal Medicine, Wan Fang Hospital, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Division of Endocrinology and Metabolism, Department of Internal Medicine, School of Medicine, College of Medicine, Taipei Medical University, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Hung SH","lastName":"Hung","firstName":"Shun-Hsing","initials":"SH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Urology, Department of Surgery, Chi Mei Hospital, Chiali, Tainan, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3485-9775"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4095019,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy","title":"Journal of asthma and allergy","essn":"1178-6965","issn":"1178-6965","nlmid":"101543450","isoabbreviation":"J Asthma Allergy"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"637738","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>While glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) show promise in improving respiratory outcomes, the comparative effectiveness of dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide/GLP-1 RA versus GLP-1 RA alone remains unclear.<h4>Objective</h4>To assess the association of tirzepatide versus semaglutide with the risk of asthma exacerbation among patients with asthma and type 2 diabetes (T2D).<h4>Methods</h4>Data from the TriNetX US Collaborative Network were used. Adults with asthma and T2D who initiated tirzepatide or semaglutide between June 01, 2022, and December 31, 2024, were included. The study period ended on December 1, 2025. The primary outcome was time to first asthma exacerbation over a 12-month follow-up. Secondary outcomes included systemic corticosteroid and short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) use. Propensity score matching was used to balance baseline covariates. Kaplan-Meier curves and Cox regression models were used to estimate comparative effectiveness.<h4>Results</h4>After 1:1 matching, 8176 patients were included in each group. The risk of asthma exacerbation was similar between tirzepatide and semaglutide (11.0% vs 11.1%; HR, 1.00; 95% CI, 0.91-1.10). This finding remained consistent across sensitivity and subgroup analyses. For secondary outcomes, tirzepatide was associated with a lower risk of SABA use (HR, 0.92; 95% CI, 0.88-0.96) and a similar risk of systemic corticosteroid use (HR, 1.01; 95% CI, 0.96-1.05) compared with semaglutide.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Among patients with asthma and T2D, no difference in the risk of asthma exacerbation between tirzepatide and semaglutide was observed. Further investigation through randomized controlled trials is warranted to clarify the potential role of GLP-1 RA-based therapies in patients with asthma and T2D.","affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacy, College of Pharmacy, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Type 2 diabetes","Glucose-dependent Insulinotropic Polypeptide","Asthma Exacerbation","Glucagon-like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonist","Semaglutide","Real-world Evidence","Tirzepatide"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JAA.S637738"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13565390"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13565390?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-14","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-07","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42748058","source":"MED","pmid":"42748058","pmcid":"PMC13580976","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13580976"]},"doi":"10.1111/pai.70488","title":"Postnatal growth and childhood asthma in preterm and full-term twins: A cohort study.","authorString":"Putri RR, Lundholm C, Mubanga M, Trillkott C, Ullemar V, Sävendahl L, Almqvist C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Putri RR","lastName":"Putri","firstName":"Resthie R","initials":"RR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6659-1937"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Lundholm C","lastName":"Lundholm","firstName":"Cecilia","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6546-3650"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Mubanga M","lastName":"Mubanga","firstName":"Mwenya","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6877-4307"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Strategic Information Unit, The Centre for Infectious Disease Research in Zambia, Lusaka, Zambia."}]}},{"fullName":"Trillkott C","lastName":"Trillkott","firstName":"Christian","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-5167-4860"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Ullemar V","lastName":"Ullemar","firstName":"Vilhelmina","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Oncology-Pathology, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Department of Gynecology and Reproductive Medicine, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Sävendahl L","lastName":"Sävendahl","firstName":"Lars","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1067-4976"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pediatric Endocrinology, Department of Women's and Children's Health (KBH), Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Pediatric Endocrinology Unit, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Almqvist C","lastName":"Almqvist","firstName":"Catarina","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Pediatric Allergy and Pulmonology Unit, Astrid Lindgren Children's Hospital, Karolinska University Hospital, Stockholm, Sweden."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6546-3650"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6659-1937"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6877-4307"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1067-4976"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-5167-4860"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4265183,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol","title":"Pediatric allergy and immunology : official publication of the European Society of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology","essn":"1399-3038","issn":"0905-6157","nlmid":"9106718","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70488","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The role of postnatal growth in asthma development in children remains unclear. We examined the association between postnatal growth pattern and childhood asthma and assessed whether this association differed between preterm and full-term children.<h4>Methods</h4>This cohort study included twins from the Child and Adolescent Twin Study in Sweden (born 1992-2003), linked to national registers. Postnatal weight and height to age 5 were obtained from child health records and standardized for age and sex (SDS). Childhood asthma was defined by parent-reported asthma at age 9 or 12. Analyses were stratified by preterm (<37 weeks) and full-term birth.<h4>Results</h4>Among 3059 children, 15.6% developed asthma, 38.3% were born preterm. On average, the asthma group had 0.16 lower weight and 0.27 lower height SDS during the first 6 months, with no growth differences between asthma and non-asthma groups thereafter. These early differences were apparent among preterm children. From the identified growth trajectories, normal birth size with subsequent normal growth occurred in 79.1% of full-term and 41.9% of preterm children. A group with low birth size followed by catch-up growth was associated with an increased odds of asthma (adjusted OR: 1.68, 95% CI: 1.16-2.45).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Postnatal growth differences between children with and without asthma were limited to the first months of life among those born preterm, with similar growth patterns thereafter up to age 5 and among full-term children. Among the identified growth trajectories, childhood asthma was most prevalent in those with low birth size and subsequent catch-up growth.","affiliation":"Department of Medical Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Karolinska Institutet, Stockholm, Sweden.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Twin Study","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Vetenskapsrådet","grantId":"2024-03265","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Vetenskapsrådet","grantId":"2023-02327","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Stiftelsen Frimurare Barnhuset i Stockholm","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Hjärt-Lungfonden","grantId":"20240974","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Sällskapet Barnavård","grantId":"2026-035","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Astma- och Allergiförbundet","grantId":"2024-0010","orderIn":0},{"agency":"H.K.H. Kronprinsessan Lovis