"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unit of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Experimental Medicine, University of Rome 'Tor Vergata', Rome, Italy. Electronic address: mario.cazzola@uniroma2.it."}]}},{"fullName":"Vaghi A","lastName":"Vaghi","firstName":"Adriano","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Former Head of Pneumology and Chief of the Department of Medicine and Rehabilitation, Guido Salvini Hospital-ASST Rhodense, Garbagnate Milanese (Milan), Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Bilò MB","lastName":"Bilò","firstName":"Maria Beatrice","initials":"MB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy Unit, Department of Internal Medicine, University Hospital AOU Delle Marche, Ancona, Italy; Department of Clinical and Molecular Sciences, Marche Polytechnic University, Ancona, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7972-8296"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9873-9695"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0658-2449"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-6535-3948"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1860-2726"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"262","journalIssueId":4260871,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109078","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Mild and moderate asthma are the most common forms of asthma, but they often remain poorly controlled due to inappropriate treatment strategies and low adherence. Formal consensus on best practices for managing mild-to-moderate asthma is still lacking.<h4>Objectives</h4>To achieve formal consensus on the management of mild-to-moderate asthma and validate the appropriateness and clinical feasibility of the recommendations in clinical practice.<h4>Methods</h4>A modified Delphi process involving a panel of respiratory experts was employed to formulate consensus statements across the following key domains identified through a cross-sectional survey administered to Italian allergists and immunologists: definition of asthma control, tools for assessment of asthma control, patient phenotyping, personalized treatment, and patient education and empowerment. The appropriateness and clinical feasibility of the agreed statements were validated with a RAND/UCLA method.<h4>Results</h4>Full consensus was reached on asthma control in mild-to-moderate asthma, defined as the absence of symptoms, exacerbations, limitation of daily activities, and the need for oral corticosteroids, together with the optimization of lung function. Assessment of control, future risk, and phenotype should be performed with appropriate tools and timing, to implement proactive management especially in patients at risk of exacerbations and disease progression.<h4>Conclusion</h4>This study provides a comprehensive, expert-driven consensus for the management of mild-to-moderate asthma, formally validated for appropriateness despite variability in real-world implementation. The findings support a patient-centric, proactive approach and offer practical recommendations to bridge the gap between guidelines and clinical practice.","affiliation":"Allergy Unit, Department of Internal Medicine, University Hospital AOU Delle Marche, Ancona, Italy. Electronic address: matteo.martini.med@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Consensus Statement","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disease Progression"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AD","qualifierName":"administration & dosage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Severity of Illness Index"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Phenotype"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Delphi Technique"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disease Management"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Italy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Patient Education as Topic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Practice Guidelines as Topic"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Patient Adherence","Consensus","Asthma Control","Mild Asthma","Asthma Phenotyping","Moderate Asthma","Mild-to-moderate Asthma"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109078"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-06","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-07","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42994888","source":"MED","pmid":"42994888","pmcid":"PMC13747491","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13747491"]},"doi":"10.1097/md.0000000000051490","title":"The influence of high body mass index on asthma: A study integrating GBD 2021 and Mendelian randomization.","authorString":"Yao X, Zhang C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yao X","lastName":"Yao","firstName":"Xiaokun","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Nanjing Jiangbei Hospital, Nanjing, Jiangsu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang C","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Chuanli","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-6146-7013"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-6146-7013"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"45","volume":"105","journalIssueId":4309929,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Medicine (Baltimore)","title":"Medicine","essn":"1536-5964","issn":"0304-5412","nlmid":"2985248R","isoabbreviation":"Medicine (Baltimore)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e51490","abstractText":"Elevated body mass index (BMI) represents a significant adjustable risk element for asthma, posing a considerable worldwide health concern. This research was designed to assess the worldwide impact of asthma due to high BMI between 1990 and 2021, forecast patterns through 2050, and use 2-sample Mendelian randomization (MR) to examine whether elevated BMI is genetically associated with asthma risk, in order to guide preventive approaches. The data for this study were obtained from the global burden of disease 2021 study and the integrative epidemiology unit OpenGWAS project. We employed decomposition analysis, Joinpoint regression, and the Bayesian age-period-cohort model to analyze asthma-related mortality and disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) by age, sex, and socio-demographic index region. Additionally, a bidirectional 2-sample MR analysis was performed to investigate the association between genetically predicted BMI and asthma risk. From 1990 to 2021, the global age-standardized mortality rate and age-standardized DALY rate for asthma attributable to high BMI declined. In contrast, the absolute number of deaths and DALYs increased by 63.9% and 45.7%, respectively. The most significant decline occurred between 1996 and 2000, while low-middle socio-demographic index regions experienced consistent increases. Projections to 2050 indicate a continued decline in age-standardized mortality rate but a slight increase in age-standardized DALY rate. Notably, females and older adults carried a higher disease burden. The MR analysis identified a positive, genetically supported association between elevated BMI and asthma risk (inverse-variance weighted odds ratio = 1.389, 95% confidence interval: 1.303-1.479). However, substantial heterogeneity across genetic variants suggests that this estimate should be interpreted with caution. The asthma burden related to high BMI is forecast to increase in the coming decades, mainly fueled by population growth, population aging, and socioeconomic inequalities. MR further supports a unidirectional, genetically supported association between higher BMI and asthma risk. These results highlight the critical necessity for implementing focused and fair public health strategies across the globe to tackle elevated BMI and reduce the associated asthma burden.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Nanjing Jiangbei Hospital, Nanjing, Jiangsu, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MO","qualifierName":"mortality"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Obesity","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Body Mass Index"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bayes Theorem"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mendelian Randomization Analysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Global Burden of Disease"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disability-Adjusted Life Years"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","body mass index","Global Burden Of Disease","Mendelian Randomization"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/MD.0000000000051490"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13747491"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13747491?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gwas","refsnp","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-06","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42969591","source":"MED","pmid":"42969591","doi":"10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_112_26","title":"Association between platelet/high-density lipoprotein cholesterol ratio and asthma in American adults: A population-based study.","authorString":"Guo Y, Zhou W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Guo Y","lastName":"Guo","firstName":"Yun","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Clinical Medicine, Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, Ningxia, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou W","lastName":"Zhou","firstName":"Wei","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, People's Hospital of Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region, Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, Ningxia, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"43","journalIssueId":4303214,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Lung India","title":"Lung India : official organ of Indian Chest Society","essn":"0974-598X","issn":"0970-2113","nlmid":"8405380","isoabbreviation":"Lung India"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"677-683","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Asthma is a chronic inflammatory disease in adults, in which systemic inflammation plays a key pathogenic role. The platelet-to-high-density lipoprotein ratio (PHR) has emerged as a biomarker of systemic inflammation. Although studied in relation to asthma, its association with adult asthma remains inadequately characterized, and evidence from large-scale epidemiological studies is limited.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to investigate the association between the PHR and asthma in adults, using data from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) 2011-2018.<h4>Methods</h4>A cross-sectional analysis was performed on 22,617 adult participants from NHANES. Asthma status was defined based on self-report, and PHR was calculated as platelet count (1,000/μL) divided by high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (mg/dL). We used multivariable logistic regression to examine the association between PHR (analyzed as both continuous and categorical variables by quartiles) and asthma. Nonlinearity was assessed using restricted cubic splines, and the robustness of findings was evaluated through a series of sensitivity analyses, including weighted regression and multiple imputation for missing data.<h4>Results</h4>Using multivariable logistic regression, we found a significant positive association between PHR and adult asthma (OR = 1.03, 95% CI: 1.01-1.05; P = 0.009). When PHR was divided into quartiles, the Q2 and Q4 groups had higher asthma risk than Q1 (risk ratios of 1.19 and 1.20, respectively). Asthma prevalence increased significantly across PHR quartiles ( P for trend <0.001). Restricted cubic spline analysis indicated a linear relationship, with the odds of asthma rising as PHR increased. These results remained robust in both weighted and sensitivity analyses.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Our findings show that higher PHR is associated with an increased prevalence of asthma, suggesting that higher PHR may be linked to asthma diagnosis in US adults.","affiliation":"School of Clinical Medicine, Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, Ningxia, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Adults","Cross-sectional study","Systemic Inflammation","Nhanes","Platelet-to-high-density Lipoprotein Cholesterol Ratio"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_112_26"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-31","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"43014016","source":"MED","pmid":"43014016","doi":"10.1016/j.intimp.2026.117667","title":"Liensinine alleviates experimental asthma by attenuating PINK1-associated mitophagy signaling and cellular senescence.","authorString":"He X, Yao D, Du Y, Zuo L, Fu S, Shang X, You M, Qian Z, Ban J, He Q.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"He X","lastName":"He","firstName":"Xiu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China. Electronic address: hexiu@gmc.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Yao D","lastName":"Yao","firstName":"Dengxiang","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Du Y","lastName":"Du","firstName":"Yiyang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zuo L","lastName":"Zuo","firstName":"Lijuan","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Fu S","lastName":"Fu","firstName":"Shuai","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shang X","lastName":"Shang","firstName":"Xuehua","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"You M","lastName":"You","firstName":"Mingdan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qian Z","lastName":"Qian","firstName":"Zhiyao","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ban J","lastName":"Ban","firstName":"Jiaqi","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China."}]}},{"fullName":"He Q","lastName":"He","firstName":"Qian","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, The Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang, 561113, China. Electronic address: 2023140020721@stu.gmc.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"Pt 1","volume":"191","journalIssueId":4303568,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int Immunopharmacol","title":"International immunopharmacology","essn":"1878-1705","issn":"1567-5769","nlmid":"100965259","isoabbreviation":"Int Immunopharmacol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"117667","abstractText":"Toluene diisocyanate (TDI) is a major cause of occupational asthma (OA) with few effective therapies. Dysregulated mitophagy signaling and cellular senescence have been implicated in airway inflammation; however, their roles in TDI-induced asthma remain unclear. The natural alkaloid liensinine (Lie) has been reported to inhibit mitophagy, but its protective potential in asthma remains unexplored. This study investigated whether Lie could alleviate experimental asthma by attenuating PINK1-associated mitophagy signaling and the related cellular senescence. We established mouse and cellular models of TDI-induced asthma. In vivo, mice received intraperitoneal Lie and intranasal adeno-associated virus (AAV) for PINK1 overexpression. In vitro, HBE135-E6E7 cells were exposed to TDI-human serum albumin (TDI-HSA) and transduced with lentivirus for PINK1 overexpression. Lie significantly mitigated airway hyperresponsiveness (AHR), inflammation, and fibrosis. Molecular docking predicted a potential interaction between Lie and PINK1. Lie attenuated TDI-induced PINK1-associated mitophagy signaling, restored mitochondrial function, and reduced senescence. PINK1 overexpression largely reversed the protective effects of Lie on mitochondrial function and attenuated its senescence-reducing effect. In a Transwell co-culture system, pretreatment of TDI-HSA-stimulated HBE135-E6E7 cells with Lie was associated with reduced proliferation, migration, and fibrotic marker expression in MRC-5 cells in the lower chamber, possibly through attenuation of TDI-HSA-induced epithelial injury. Lie pretreatment attenuated PINK1-associated mitophagy signaling and the related cellular senescence in a TDI-induced experimental asthma model.","affiliation":"School of Public Health, Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Monitoring and Disease Control, Ministry of Education, Guizhou Medical University, Guiyang 561113, China. Electronic address: hexiu@gmc.edu.cn.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cellular senescence","Liensinine","Pink1","Mitophagy","Toluene Diisocyanate","Experimental Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intimp.2026.117667"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-11","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-12","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42779388","source":"MED","pmid":"42779388","pmcid":"PMC13601965","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13601965"]},"doi":"10.1111/pai.70480","title":"Update on Pediatric asthma management: Recent advances.","authorString":"Dhochak N, Robinson PFM, Saglani S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Dhochak N","lastName":"Dhochak","firstName":"Nitin","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3676-8930"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Great Ormond Street Hospital, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Robinson PFM","lastName":"Robinson","firstName":"Polly F M","initials":"PFM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2463-3024"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Royal Brompton Hospital, London, UK."},{"affiliation":"Evelina London Children's Hospital, St Thomas' Hospital, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Saglani S","lastName":"Saglani","firstName":"Sejal","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5192-6418"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Royal Brompton Hospital, London, UK."},{"affiliation":"National Heart & Lung Institute, Imperial College London & Imperial Centre for Paediatrics & Child Health, London, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5192-6418"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3676-8930"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2463-3024"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4265183,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol","title":"Pediatric allergy and immunology : official publication of the European Society of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology","essn":"1399-3038","issn":"0905-6157","nlmid":"9106718","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Allergy Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70480","abstractText":"Asthma remains the commonest chronic pediatric respiratory disease and a major cause of childhood morbidity worldwide. Recent advances in pediatric asthma management shift the focus from symptom relief alone towards early control of airway inflammation, exacerbation prevention, and personalizing biomarkers to target biologics. This review highlights the growing role of anti-inflammatory reliever [AIR] therapy and maintenance-and-reliever therapy [MART], both of which incorporate inhaled corticosteroids [ICS] with bronchodilator treatment to reduce reliance on short-acting β<sub>2</sub>-agonists [SABA]. Pediatric clinical trials demonstrate that strategies including AIR only and MART, compared to SABA reliever, reduce acute asthma attack burden in some disease subgroups while further large trials are underway. For severe pediatric asthma, advances in understanding type 2 airway inflammation have led to the introduction of biologics targeting key inflammatory pathways, including IgE, interleukin (IL)-5, IL-5 receptor, IL-4/IL-13 signaling, and thymic stromal lymphopoietin. Adult trials have shown reductions in asthma attacks with all currently licensed biologics. Although pediatric evidence remains more limited than in adults, we summarize the current evidence of efficacy in childhood severe asthma. Biomarker-guided selection using blood eosinophils, fractional exhaled nitric oxide, and serum IgE is increasingly important, yet optimal pediatric biomarkers of response remain uncertain. Current challenges include gaps in child-specific biomarker validation and uncertainty regarding treatment duration and discontinuation strategies. Overall, contemporary pediatric asthma management is moving towards precision medicine, integrating anti-inflammatory reliever strategies and targeted biologics to improve disease control, minimize exacerbations, and preserve long-term lung health.","affiliation":"Great Ormond Street Hospital, London, UK.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Eosinophils"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Anti-Inflammatory Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biomarkers","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Biological products","Biomarkers","Pediatric","Disease Management"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Adrenal Cortex Hormones","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Anti-Inflammatory Agents","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Anti-Asthmatic Agents","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Biomarkers","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/pai.70480"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601965"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601965?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42838722","source":"MED","pmid":"42838722","doi":"10.2500/aap.2026.47.260079","title":"Ultraviolet irradiation and the home microbiome in childhood asthma: An exploratory environmental analysis.","authorString":"Ware K, Ollberding N, Duan Q, Haslam D, Phipatanakul W, Glazman M, Bernstein JA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ware K","lastName":"Ware","firstName":"Kaleb","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Division of Rheumatology, Allergy and Immunology, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, Ohio."}]}},{"fullName":"Ollberding N","lastName":"Ollberding","firstName":"Nicholas","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Biostatistics and Epidemiology, Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center, Cincinnati, Ohio."}]}},{"fullName":"Duan Q","lastName":"Duan","firstName":"Qing","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Biostatistics and Epidemiology, Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center, Cincinnati, Ohio."}]}},{"fullName":"Haslam D","lastName":"Haslam","firstName":"David","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Biostatistics and Epidemiology, Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center, Cincinnati, Ohio."}]}},{"fullName":"Phipatanakul W","lastName":"Phipatanakul","firstName":"Wanda","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Immunology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, Massachusetts, and."}]}},{"fullName":"Glazman M","lastName":"Glazman","firstName":"Mark","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"General Innovations and Goods, Inc., Columbus, Ohio."}]}},{"fullName":"Bernstein JA","lastName":"Bernstein","firstName":"Jonathan A","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Division of Rheumatology, Allergy and Immunology, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, Ohio."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"47","journalIssueId":4300412,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Allergy Asthma Proc","title":"Allergy and asthma proceedings","essn":"1539-6304","issn":"1088-5412","nlmid":"9603640","isoabbreviation":"Allergy Asthma Proc"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"394-398","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Ultraviolet (UV) filtration units installed into central heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems have been shown to modify indoor environmental microbiomes (EM), but it remains unclear whether central UV air filtration systems as a targeted intervention alters the EM sufficiently to modify asthma outcomes. <b>Objective:</b> The objective was to investigate changes in the microbiome from dust samples collected before and after installation of a central UV filtration system in homes of children with mild-moderate persistent asthma. <b>Methods:</b> Enrolled pediatric subjects with asthma were randomized to receive UV filtration or sham devices in their heating, ventilation, and air conditioning units. Dust samples were collected from the furnace filters and each child's bedroom inflow air ducts from the first 20 homes randomized at the two largest recruiting sites at the time of device placement and at study completion (12 months), along with periodic measurements of asthma outcomes markers by using the Composite Asthma Severity Index. Microbial DNA from each paired dust sample underwent shotgun metagenomic sequencing, and taxonomic profiles were generated with MetaPhlAn 4. Associations between changes in bacterial species abundance and changes in asthma severity were assessed with Microbiome Multivariable Associations with Linear Models in R. <b>Results:</b> A total of 14 paired dust samples (7 from the UV homes and 7 from the sham homes) from inflow air ducts with sufficient quantity of dust were included for EM analysis. Within the UV filtration homes, false discovery rate adjusted q-values < 0.05 identified associations between worsening asthma severity and reduced relative abundance of common gastrointestinal commensals, such as Bacteroides and Bifidobacterium, previously linked to a reduced risk of allergic disease and asthma; no associations at this threshold were observed in the sham homes. <b>Conclusion:</b> An exploratory study of UV air filtration units installed in homes of children with asthma identified hypothesis-generating associations between bacterial species changes and asthma severity; because the study was underpowered for between-arm comparisons, these findings do not establish that UV air filtration caused the microbiome changes and should not be interpreted as confirmatory of a UV-specific effect.Clinical trial NCT02715375, <ext-link xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"uri\" xlink:href=\"http://www.clinicaltrials.gov\">www.clinicaltrials.gov</ext-link>.","affiliation":"From the Division of Rheumatology, Allergy and Immunology, College of Medicine, University of Cincinnati, Cincinnati, Ohio.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"K24 AI106822","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Dust","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AN","qualifierName":"analysis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Housing"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Ultraviolet Rays"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Air Pollution, Indoor","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Environmental Exposure"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child, Preschool"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Microbiota","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"RE","qualifierName":"radiation effects"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Dust","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2