":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1807-3246"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Locum GP, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Masekela R","lastName":"Masekela","firstName":"Refiloe","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Paediatrics and Child Health, School of Medicine, University of KwaZulu Natal, South Africa."}]}},{"fullName":"Pitrez PM","lastName":"Pitrez","firstName":"Paulo Marcio","initials":"PM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Instituto Ceos, Porto Alegre, Brazil and Hospital Santa Casa de Porto Alegre, Porto Alegre, Brazil."},{"affiliation":"Instituto Ceos, Porto Alegre, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Salvi S","lastName":"Salvi","firstName":"Sundeep","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pulmocare Research and Education (PURE) Foundation, Symbiosis International University, Pune, India."},{"affiliation":"Symbiosis Medical College for Women & Symbiosis University Hospital and Research Centre, Symbiosis International (Deemed University), Pune, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Yorgancioğlu A","lastName":"Yorgancioğlu","firstName":"Arzu","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Celal Bayar University Medical Faculty Department of Pulmonology, Manisa, Turkey."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang M","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Min","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5026-0099"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine, Shanghai General Hospital, Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine, Shanghai, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Brusselle G","lastName":"Brusselle","firstName":"Guy","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7021-8505"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Medicine, Ghent University Hospital, Ghent, Belgium."},{"affiliation":"Departments of Epidemiology and Respiratory Medicine, Erasmus Medical Center Rotterdam, Rotterdam, the Netherlands."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5096-0614"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6731-9452"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7021-8505"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8454-0087"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1807-3246"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4645-5209"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6695-6350"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5026-0099"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4309342,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Eur Respir J","title":"The European respiratory journal","essn":"1399-3003","issn":"0903-1936","nlmid":"8803460","isoabbreviation":"Eur Respir J"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2602304","abstractText":"The Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Strategy Report provides a comprehensive, annually updated evidence-based approach to asthma diagnosis, assessment, treatment, and prevention across the lifespan. Its central approach is personalized, iterative care based on the Assess-Adjust-Review cycle for both symptom control and reduction of future risk (including severe exacerbations, persistent airflow limitation, and treatment-related harm).GINA emphasizes objective confirmation of diagnosis wherever possible, and regular assessment of lung function, inhaler technique, adherence, multimorbidity, risk factors (including Type-2 biomarkers), and patient goals and preferences. GINA strongly recommends that all adults, adolescents, children and most preschoolers with asthma should receive inhaled corticosteroid (ICS)-containing treatment. GINA recommends against short-acting beta<sub>2</sub>-agonist (SABA)-only treatment. For adults/adolescents, GINA Track 1 using low-dose ICS-formoterol, an anti-inflammatory reliever (AIR), either as-needed-only (AIR-only) or as maintenance-and-reliever therapy (MART), is preferred because of its substantial reduction in severe exacerbations compared with SABA-based regimens, and its simplicity. Age-specific treatment pathways in children also emphasize ICS-containing therapy and exacerbation prevention.For difficult-to-treat and severe asthma, GINA provides structured guidance, emphasizing diagnostic verification, optimization of modifiable factors, inflammatory phenotyping, biologic therapy selection, and (as for all patients) systemic corticosteroid stewardship. Acute exacerbation care incorporates long-term preventive treatment, including post-discharge reassessment of management and action plan.Key 2026 updates include expanded AIR options for adults and children, revised guidance on severe asthma and selection of biologic treatment, and substantial updates to acute asthma management.GINA integrates evidence-based management with shared decision-making, self-management education, implementation, and global advocacy to reduce preventable asthma morbidity and mortality.","affiliation":"Woolcock Institute of Medical Research and Macquarie Medical School, Macquarie University; University of Sydney, Sydney, Australia helen.reddel@woolcock.org.au.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.02304-2026"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-07","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42785448","source":"MED","pmid":"42785448","doi":"10.1016/j.jevs.2026.106154","title":"Inflammatory phenotype and response to intradermal skin testing are not predictive of equine asthma diagnosis.","authorString":"Hunyadi LM, Hyde SC, Sundman EA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hunyadi LM","lastName":"Hunyadi","firstName":"L M","initials":"LM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Advanced Equine Diagnostics, PO Box 981, Kapaau, HI, 96755, USA; School of Veterinary Medicine, Texas Tech University, 7671 Evans Dr, Amarillo, TX, 79106, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hyde SC","lastName":"Hyde","firstName":"S C","initials":"SC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Veterinary Medicine, Texas Tech University, 7671 Evans Dr, Amarillo, TX, 79106, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sundman EA","lastName":"Sundman","firstName":"E A","initials":"EA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Advanced Equine Diagnostics, PO Box 981, Kapaau, HI, 96755, USA; School of Veterinary Medicine, Texas Tech University, 7671 Evans Dr, Amarillo, TX, 79106, USA. Electronic address: LHUNYADI@ttu.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7949-4266"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4253702,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Equine Vet Sci","title":"Journal of equine veterinary science","issn":"0737-0806","nlmid":"8216840","isoabbreviation":"J Equine Vet Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"106154","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The role of inflammation, allergy, and air quality in the pathogenesis of equine asthma has not yet been fully elucidated.<h4>Aims/objectives</h4>To evaluate serum inflammatory cytokines, intradermal skin testing (IDT), and air quality in asthmatic horses. It was hypothesized that there would be an association between environmental allergies and poor air quality with asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>52 client-owned horses with clinical signs consistent with asthma were enrolled in this cross-sectional study. Horses were diagnosed with asthma based on the results of bronchoalveolar fluid (BALF) cytology. Blood samples were analyzed for 23 inflammatory cytokines. IDT was conducted on a subset of 31 horses. Aerosol mass fraction concentrations were measured and converted to air quality index (AQI).<h4>Results</h4>Cytologic diagnoses of asthma were made in 34/52 horses. All non-asthmatic horses that underwent IDT testing showed a severe response to ≥1 allergen (100%), while 60% of asthmatic horses showed the same response. There was a statistically significant overrepresentation of mastocytic asthma at sites with unhealthy AQI (expected 10.3%, observed 24.1%; p = 0.0213). This study failed to find any differences between asthma phenotypes for IDT results or identify inflammatory cytokines in serum as biomarkers for allergic airway disease.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Diagnostic testing for environmental allergies and serum biomarkers were not predictive of asthma phenotypes. The association between the occurrence of equine asthma and poor air quality is consistent with results found in humans.","affiliation":"Advanced Equine Diagnostics, PO Box 981, Kapaau, HI, 96755, USA; School of Veterinary Medicine, Texas Tech University, 7671 Evans Dr, Amarillo, TX, 79106, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Allergy","Asthma","Intradermal Skin Testing","Serum Cytokines"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jevs.2026.106154"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-26","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42540843","source":"MED","pmid":"42540843","pmcid":"PMC13426496","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13426496"]},"doi":"10.2147/copd.s609495","title":"Routine Blood Biomarkers and Lung Function in Asthma-COPD Overlap versus Asthma-Only Among Hospitalized Asthma Patients: A Retrospective Cross-Sectional Study.","authorString":"Xie M, Jin X, Li H, Tian L, Wang S, Zhang Y, Wang T, Wang S, Wang H, Yang X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xie M","lastName":"Xie","firstName":"Mei","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Master Training Station, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Geriatric and Special Needs Medicine Integrated Ward, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Jin X","lastName":"Jin","firstName":"Xiaofei","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Geriatric and Special Needs Medicine Integrated Ward, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li H","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Hui","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Geriatric and Special Needs Medicine Integrated Ward, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tian L","lastName":"Tian","firstName":"Lei","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Clinical Medicine, Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang S","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Shaojin","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Yating","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang T","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Ting","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang S","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Shuni","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang H","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Hongyi","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Clinical Medicine, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences and Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, People's Republic of China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Sciences, Medical Laboratory of Innovation and Education Co., Ltd., Changsha, Hunan, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yang X","lastName":"Yang","firstName":"Xiaoyan","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Key Laboratory of Ningxia Stem Cell and Regenerative Medicine, Institute of Medical Sciences, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"21","journalIssueId":4091442,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Chron Obstruct Pulmon Dis","title":"International journal of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","essn":"1178-2005","issn":"1176-9106","nlmid":"101273481","isoabbreviation":"Int J Chron Obstruct Pulmon Dis"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"609495","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To compare routine blood biomarkers, lung function, and medication-associated biomarker patterns between hospitalized asthma patients classified as having asthma-COPD overlap (ACO) and those with asthma-only.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective cross-sectional analysis included 505 hospitalized asthma patients admitted between January 2020 and December 2024, comprising 190 with ACO and 315 with asthma-only. Disease history, pulmonary function parameters, complete blood cell counts, differential percentages, immunoglobulin E, and medication exposure were extracted from electronic medical records and analyzed using complete cases. Multivariable logistic regression, multivariable linear regression, Spearman correlation, and receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analyses were used to evaluate associations and discriminatory performance. Medication analyses were exploratory because treatment was not randomized.<h4>Results</h4>Compared with asthma-only patients, patients with ACO were older, had a lower prevalence of allergy history, used inhaled corticosteroids, long-acting beta2-agonists, and long-acting muscarinic antagonists more frequently, and had worse airflow limitation and lower diffusing capacity. Higher neutrophil percentage (adjusted odds ratio: 2.15, 95% confidence interval: 1.62-2.85, p<0.001) and lower lymphocyte percentage (adjusted odds ratio: 0.51, 95% confidence interval: 0.39-0.67, p<0.001) were independently associated with ACO after Bonferroni correction. In ACO patients, inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting beta2-agonist exposure was nominally associated with higher neutrophil-related and lower lymphocyte-related measures, but these observational findings should not be interpreted as treatment effects. FEV1/FVC showed the best discrimination for ACO (area under the curve [AUC]: 0.696), whereas neutrophil percentage showed modest complementary discrimination (AUC: 0.596).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Within this hospitalized asthma cohort, ACO was associated with older age, more severe airflow limitation, impaired diffusing capacity, and a more neutrophil-dominant systemic inflammatory profile than asthma-only. Routine blood biomarkers, particularly neutrophil and lymphocyte percentages, may complement pulmonary function parameters in identifying ACO, but their standalone diagnostic performance was modest. Medication-associated biomarker differences were exploratory and require prospective validation.","affiliation":"Department of Master Training Station, General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University, Yinchuan, People's Republic of China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Lung","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neutrophils"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Respiratory Function Tests"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Forced Expiratory Volume"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hospitalization"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retrospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biomarkers","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma-Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Overlap Syndrome","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Neutrophils","Lung function","Cross-sectional study","Asthma-copd Overlap","Asthma-only","Routine Blood Biomarkers"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Biomarkers","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/COPD.S609495"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13426496"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13426496?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42764096","source":"MED","pmid":"42764096","doi":"10.1016/j.jaci.2026.09.005","title":"Nasal epithelial transcriptome-wide isoform-level expression and asthma endotypes in youth.","authorString":"Liu T, Yue M, Gaietto K, Hu H, Ye X, Rosser FJ, Han YY, Chen W, Celedón JC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu T","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Tianhao","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa; Tsinghua Medicine, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yue M","lastName":"Yue","firstName":"Molin","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa; Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, School of Public Health, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Gaietto K","lastName":"Gaietto","firstName":"Kristina","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu H","lastName":"Hu","firstName":"Haoran","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, School of Public Health, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye X","lastName":"Ye","firstName":"Xiangyu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Rosser FJ","lastName":"Rosser","firstName":"Franziska J","initials":"FJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Han YY","lastName":"Han","firstName":"Yueh-Ying","initials":"YY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen W","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Wei","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa; Department of Biostatistics and Health Data Science, School of Public Health, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa."}]}},{"fullName":"Celedón JC","lastName":"Celedón","firstName":"Juan C","initials":"JC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6139-5320"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa. Electronic address: juan.celedon@chp.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6139-5320"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4251904,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol","title":"The Journal of allergy and clinical immunology","essn":"1097-6825","issn":"0091-6749","nlmid":"1275002","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S0091-6749(26)00642-1","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Previous transcriptome-wide association studies of asthma lacked analyses of isoform expression.<h4>Objective</h4>We sought to identify isoforms associated with asthma endotypes in children and adolescents.<h4>Methods</h4>Meta-analysis of association between isoform- and gene-level nasal epithelial expression and T<sub>H</sub>2-high or T<sub>H</sub>17-high asthma (with T<sub>H</sub>2-low/T<sub>H</sub>17-low asthma as the reference group) in 459 youths with asthma aged 8-20 years in 3 studies: Epigenetic Variation and Childhood Asthma in Puerto Ricans (EVA-PR), Stress and Treatment Response in Puerto Rican and African American Children With Asthma (STAR), and Vitamin D Kids Asthma (VDKA). Genes or isoforms with the same direction of association with an endotype in all cohorts and a meta-analysis false discovery rate-adjusted P < .01 were considered to have significantly differential expression.<h4>Results</h4>In total, 6,607 isoforms encoded by 5,168 genes were associated with T<sub>H</sub>2-high asthma; 1,019 (19.7%) of these 5,168 genes were only identified in the isoform analysis, including CTNND1, CREM, TAOK2, and AIM1. A total of 4,430 isoforms encoded by 3,643 genes were associated with T<sub>H</sub>17-high asthma; 371 (10.2%) of these genes were only identified in the isoform analysis, including PSAP, FAM65B, and CFLAR. While top findings in the gene- and isoform-level analyses often overlapped, the isoforms of many genes showed differential isoform usage. In T<sub>H</sub>2-high asthma, 499 genes (encoding 731 isoforms) altered isoform usage, including CDH26, IL1RL1, MAP2K6, and SLC39A8. In T<sub>H</sub>17-high asthma, 113 genes (encoding 160 isoforms) altered isoform usage, including SBNO2, PSMB8, and NCOA7.<h4>Conclusions</h4>An isoform-level analysis complemented a gene-level analysis of expression and identified novel genes for asthma endotypes.","affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, UPMC Children's Hospital of Pittsburgh, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, Pa; Tsinghua Medicine, Tsinghua University, Beijing, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NHLBI","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Childhood Asthma","Isoform Expression","Asthma Endotypes","Nasal Epithelium","Transcriptome-wide Association"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaci.2026.09.005"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-16","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-21","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42882216","source":"MED","pmid":"42882216","doi":"10.3390/jcm15197757","title":"From Quiet Asthma to Clinical Remission: Can Optimized Maximal Inhaled Therapy Achieve Biologic-Free Remission in Difficult-to-Treat and Severe Asthma?","authorString":"Quaranta VN, Dragonieri S, Manzetti G, Tomasello A, Benfante A, Scichilone N, Rogliani P, Carpagnano GE.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Quaranta VN","lastName":"Quaranta","firstName":"Vitaliano Nicola","initials":"VN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9920-780X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Biomedicine and Neurosciences, Institute of Respiratory Disease, University of Bari \"Aldo Moro\", 70124 Bari, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Dragonieri S","lastName":"Dragonieri","firstName":"Silvano","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1563-6864"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Biomedicine and Neurosciences, Institute of Respiratory Disease, University of Bari \"Aldo Moro\", 70124 Bari, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Manzetti G","lastName":"Manzetti","firstName":"Gianmarco","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8265-7959"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unit of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Experimental Medicine, University of Rome \"Tor Vergata\", 00133 Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Tomasello A","lastName":"Tomasello","firstName":"Alessandra","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-8508-7728"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, PROMISE Department, Paolo Giaccone University Hospital, University of Palermo, 90127 Palermo, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Benfante A","lastName":"Benfante","firstName":"Alida","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8609-0814"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, PROMISE Department, Paolo Giaccone University Hospital, University of Palermo, 90127 Palermo, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Scichilone N","lastName":"Scichilone","firstName":"Nicola","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6400-6573"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, PROMISE Department, Paolo Giaccone University Hospital, University of Palermo, 90127 Palermo, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Rogliani P","lastName":"Rogliani","firstName":"Paola","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7801-5040"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unit of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Experimental Medicine, University of Rome \"Tor Vergata\", 00133 Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Carpagnano GE","lastName":"Carpagnano","firstName":"Giovanna Elisiana","initials":"GE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Biomedicine and Neurosciences, Institute of Respiratory Disease, University of Bari \"Aldo Moro\", 70124 Bari, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6400-6573"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7801-5040"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9920-780X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8265-7959"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8609-0814"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1563-6864"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-8508-7728"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"19","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4289908,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","title":"Journal of clinical medicine","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383","nlmid":"101606588","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"7757","abstractText":"Clinical remission, defined as sustained multidomain disease stability encompassing symptom control, freedom from exacerbations and from maintenance oral corticosteroid use, and stable or improved lung function, has emerged as a major therapeutic goal in severe asthma, largely driven by the success of biologic therapies. Consequently, remission is often regarded as an outcome primarily achievable through biologic treatment, whereas the potential contribution of optimized inhaled triple therapy has received comparatively less attention. This narrative review examines the evidence supporting biologic-free clinical remission across difficult-to-treat and severe asthma populations and explores the mechanisms that may contribute to sustained multidomain disease stability during optimized inhaled triple therapy. Importantly, we distinguish remission achieved after correction of previously unrecognized modifiable factors-which may reflect successful management of difficult-to-treat asthma-from on-treatment remission occurring in patients in whom severe asthma had already been established after appropriate treatment optimization. We review contemporary remission definitions, the biological rationale for remission achieved during optimized inhaled therapy, and evidence from historical cohorts, national registries, randomized clinical trials, post hoc analyses, and prospective real-world studies. Collectively, these data indicate that optimized inhaled triple therapy can be associated with clinically meaningful multidomain disease-stability states and, when established remission criteria are fulfilled, clinical remission, although the strength of evidence specifically in patients fulfilling current severe-asthma criteria remains more limited. Particular emphasis is placed on the contribution of small-airway dysfunction, the evolution of the quiet asthma concept as a proposed small-airway remission phenotype, and the potential roles of pharmacological synergy, improved peripheral drug deposition, and restoration of distal airway homeostasis. We also discuss the durability of remission, the need for continued multidomain follow-up, and the clinical and health-economic implications of achieving remission without biologic therapy. Current evidence suggests that optimized inhaled triple therapy should be considered not only a key component of severe asthma management but also a potential remission-oriented strategy in appropriately selected patients, including those who may achieve remission without subsequent biologic escalation. Future research should identify the phenotypes most likely to achieve biologic-free remission, clarify the underlying mechanisms, and prospectively validate remission-oriented inhaled treatment strategies.","affiliation":"Department of Translational Biomedicine and Neurosciences, Institute of Respiratory Disease, University of Bari \"Aldo Moro\", 70124 Bari, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Severe asthma","Triple Therapy","Difficult-to-treat Asthma","Clinical Remission","Inhaled Therapy","Biologic-free Remission","Small-airway Dysfunction","Quiet Asthma"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15197757"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-20","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42909753","source":"MED","pmid":"42909753","doi":"10.1007/s11010-026-05777-7","title":"Targeting STING to attenuate autophagy-mediated airway remodeling in asthma.","authorString":"Chen L, Liu S, Feng Y, Cai X, Cheng Q, Dai B, Shang Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen L","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Li","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Si","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China."}]}},{"fullName":"Feng Y","lastName":"Feng","firstName":"Yong","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cai X","lastName":"Cai","firstName":"Xuxu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cheng Q","lastName":"Cheng","firstName":"Qi","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dai B","lastName":"Dai","firstName":"Bing","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China. 18940258478@126.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Shang Y","lastName":"Shang","firstName":"Yunxiao","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China. lchen85@cmu.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4285147,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Cell Biochem","title":"Molecular and cellular biochemistry","essn":"1573-4919","issn":"0300-8177","nlmid":"0364456","isoabbreviation":"Mol Cell Biochem"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Stimulator of interferon genes (STING) is critical for antiviral and intracellular antibacterial immune defense and is involved in pulmonary fibrotic diseases. However, the role of STING in regulating autophagy and airway remodeling in asthma remains unclear. An ovalbumin (OVA)-induced mouse model of asthma airway remodeling was established. Expression levels of STING and autophagy-related gene 5 (ATG5) in lung tissues were detected. The effects of STING inhibitor C-176 on autophagy and airway remodeling were evaluated in vivo. In human bronchial epithelial BEAS-2B cells stimulated with interleukin (IL)-13, the relationships among STING, autophagy, and cellular fibrosis were investigated in vitro. In OVA-challenged mice, C-176, chloroquine (CQ), and dexamethasone (DX) reduced airway hyperresponsiveness and eosinophil counts. C-176 and CQ alleviated inflammatory cell infiltration, mucus secretion, and collagen deposition, and suppressed autophagy and fibrosis in airway epithelium. In IL-13-stimulated BEAS-2B cells, C-176, ATG5 inhibition, or PERK pathway inhibition downregulated autophagy and fibrosis‑related markers. STING upregulation in airway epithelial cells promotes autophagy and subsequent airway remodeling in asthma. Inhibition of STING or autophagy attenuates these pathological changes, indicating a potential therapeutic target for asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, No. 36, Sanhao Street, Heping District, Shenyang, 110004, Liaoning Province, P. R. China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","