sity Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK.; School of Clinical Experimental Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, Southampton, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Vorobeva E","lastName":"Vorobeva","firstName":"Elena","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) Southampton Biomedical Research Centre at University Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK.; School of Clinical Experimental Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, Southampton, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Haitchi HM","lastName":"Haitchi","firstName":"Hans Michael","initials":"HM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) Southampton Biomedical Research Centre at University Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK.; School of Clinical Experimental Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, Southampton, UK; Respiratory Medicine Department, University Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK.; Institute for Life Sciences, University of Southampton, Southampton, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Graham N","lastName":"Graham","firstName":"Nikki","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Human Development and Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, Southampton, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Kurukulaaratchy RJ","lastName":"Kurukulaaratchy","firstName":"Ramesh J","initials":"RJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) Southampton Biomedical Research Centre at University Hospital Southampton NHS Foundation Trust, Southampton, UK.; School of Clinical Experimental Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, University of Southampton, Southampton, UK; The David Hide Asthma and Allergy Research Centre, St Mary's Hospital, Newport, Isle of Wight, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Korošec P","lastName":"Korošec","firstName":"Peter","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University Clinic of Respiratory and Allergic Diseases Golnik, Golnik, Slovenia; Faculty of Pharmacy, University of Ljubljana, Ljubljana, Slovenia."}]}},{"fullName":"Kopač P","lastName":"Kopač","firstName":"Peter","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University Clinic of Respiratory and Allergic Diseases Golnik, Golnik, Slovenia; Faculty of Medicine, University of Ljubljana, Ljubljana, Slovenia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2596-4952"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4277025,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol","title":"Annals of allergy, asthma & immunology : official publication of the American College of Allergy, Asthma, & Immunology","essn":"1534-4436","issn":"1081-1206","nlmid":"9503580","isoabbreviation":"Ann Allergy Asthma Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S1081-1206(26)01271-8","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Higher basal serum tryptase (BST) levels have been associated with increased asthma severity that correlated with active tryptase allele number and was linked to poor clinical responses to anti-IgE treatment (omalizumab). However, the commonest cause of elevated BST is hereditary α-tryptasemia (HαT) caused by increased α-tryptase-encoding Tryptase alpha/beta 1 (TPSAB1) copy number, and its relationship to omalizumab response in asthma remains unclear.<h4>Objective</h4>Our aim was to assess the relative contribution of tryptase genotype and HαT to the clinical efficacy of omalizumab treatment in patients with severe allergic asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>A cohort (N=181) of severe allergic asthma patients treated with omalizumab from 2 tertiary referral centres in Slovenia and the UK underwent tryptase genotyping, and clinical features were subsequently compared between groups with and without HαT.<h4>Results</h4>Limited benefit from omalizumab therapy was demonstrated in 22.7% of patients. Overall, HαT was identified in 8.3% of patients with severe allergic asthma treated with omalizumab. HαT was found in 20% of individuals with poor clinical responses to omalizumab, compared to 5% of those with positive responses (OR = 4.6 [95% CI 1.5-12.3]; P = .0068). In multivariable analysis, adjusted for study site and other covariates, HαT remained independently associated with poor response to omalizumab (OR = 4.56; [95% CI, 1.24-16.74]; P = .022). Among patients with HαT, 4 of 15 had BST below 8 ng/mL.<h4>Conclusion</h4>HαT is associated with poorer clinical response to anti-IgE treatment in patients with severe allergic asthma.","affiliation":"University Clinic of Respiratory and Allergic Diseases Golnik, Golnik, Slovenia; Biotechnical Faculty, University of Ljubljana, Ljubljana, Slovenia; Faculty of Medicine, University of Maribor, Maribor, Slovenia;. Electronic address: matija.rijavec@klinika-golnik.si.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Slovenian Research and Innovation Agency","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute for Health and Care Research","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases Division of Intramural Research","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Response","Tryptase","Corticosteroids","Omalizumab","Severe Allergic Asthma","Hereditary Α-tryptasemia","Tryptase Alpha/beta 1 (Tpsab1)"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.anai.2026.10.014"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-15","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-15","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1333461","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-10923679/v1","title":"Macrophage characteristics do not correlate with clinical outcomes in exacerbations of neutrophilic asthma","authorString":"Joy S, Sun R, Bou-Aoun N, Nishizawa T, Wang S, McGovern TK, Morozan A, Diab N, Martin J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Joy S","lastName":"Joy","firstName":"Sydney","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Sun R","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Rui","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Bou-Aoun N","lastName":"Bou-Aoun","firstName":"Nour","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Nishizawa T","lastName":"Nishizawa","firstName":"Tomotaka","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Wang S","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Sadie","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"McGovern TK","lastName":"McGovern","firstName":"Toby","initials":"TK"},{"fullName":"Morozan A","lastName":"Morozan","firstName":"Arina","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Diab N","lastName":"Diab","firstName":"Nermin","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Martin J","lastName":"Martin","firstName":"James","initials":"J"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Airway inflammation in acute exacerbations of asthma is frequently neutrophilic. Macrophages have the potential to promote inflammation or to accelerate resolution of inflammation, however, little is known about their contribution to the pathogenesis of asthma. The aim of the study was to explore the relationship between sputum macrophages in exacerbations of neutrophilic and paucigranulocytic asthma with clinical outcomes. Paucigranulocytic (PA; n = 18) and neutrophilic (NA; n = 21) asthmatic subjects were recruited upon an exacerbation of asthma. Clinical data [lung function, exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO), and the Asthma Control Questionnaire (ACQ-6)] and sputum were collected at presentation and on 14- and 28-days. Sputum macrophage subtypes were characterized by flow cytometry. Sputum cells were further assessed by bulk RNA sequencing. Both PA and NA groups displayed impaired lung function upon exacerbation that improved by day 28. Sputum monocytes and alveolar macrophages were significantly elevated upon exacerbation among NA participants compared to PA participants. However, neither macrophage number nor immunophenotype correlated with any clinical outcome assessed. These findings were further supported by bulk RNA sequencing as macrophage enrichment did not correlate with clinical outcomes at the time of exacerbation. Macrophage characteristics do not correlate with clinical outcomes during exacerbations or during recovery, regardless of asthma phenotype.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10923679/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-05","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1333461","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-10-04","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42727290","source":"MED","pmid":"42727290","doi":"10.1016/j.resmer.2026.101321","title":"Evolution of the level of eosinophilic asthma in asthmatic children over time.","authorString":"Tesnière L, Couderc L, Grosjean J, Marguet C, Petat H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tesnière L","lastName":"Tesnière","firstName":"Laetitia","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Univ Rouen Normandie, Dynamicure INSERM UMR 1311, CHU Rouen, Department of Paediatrics and adolescent Medicine, F-76000, Rouen, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Couderc L","lastName":"Couderc","firstName":"Laure","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Univ Rouen Normandie, Dynamicure INSERM UMR 1311, CHU Rouen, Department of Paediatrics and adolescent Medicine, F-76000, Rouen, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Grosjean J","lastName":"Grosjean","firstName":"Julien","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Univ Rouen Normandie, INSERM U1142, Medical Informatics Department, CHU Rouen, 37 Bd Gambetta, 76000, Rouen, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Marguet C","lastName":"Marguet","firstName":"Christophe","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Univ Rouen Normandie, Dynamicure INSERM UMR 1311, CHU Rouen, Department of Paediatrics and adolescent Medicine, F-76000, Rouen, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Petat H","lastName":"Petat","firstName":"Hortense","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2843-871X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Univ Rouen Normandie, Dynamicure INSERM UMR 1311, CHU Rouen, Department of Paediatrics and adolescent Medicine, F-76000, Rouen, France. Electronic address: hortense.petat1@univ-rouen.fr."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2843-871X"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"90","journalIssueId":4248123,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med Res","title":"Respiratory medicine and research","essn":"2590-0412","issn":"2590-0412","nlmid":"101746324","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101321","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Asthma is the most common chronic disease in children. Advances in knowledge of asthma pathophysiology have led to the description of endotypes including: allergic eosinophilic asthma, non-allergic eosinophilic asthma (grouped under the term T2-high) and neutrophilic asthma (T2-low). Eosinophilic asthma is poorly described in children, and clinical practice suggests an increase in its prevalence over time. The aim of our study was to evaluate the evolution of the proportion of hyper-eosinophilic asthma in the pediatric population over time, and to characterize this population.<h4>Patients and methods</h4>We conducted a single-center retrospective study at the University Hospital of Rouen, France, between 1995 and 2021: 1041 children aged 0-17 years, followed for asthma in our tertiary center, in which eosinophil blood count was prescribed, were included.<h4>Results</h4>Between 1995 and 2021, we found no change in hyper-eosinophilia levels in asthmatic children. We found that blood eosinophilia > 400 cells/μL was associated with allergy, male gender and the 6-11 age group. Biologic therapies were significantly associated with hyper-eosinophilia (p < 0.001).<h4>Discussion</h4>Our results suggest that we repeat more eosinophilia assays during follow-up of an asthmatic patient, giving the clinical impression of higher hyper-eosinophilia. The three biologic therapies currently approved for young children target T2-high asthma. A new monoclonal antibody targeting TSLP is available in children over 12 years old; it is effective in T2-low asthma, according to initial studies. This would be an interesting prospect for young children with severe T2-low, for whom no biotherapy is currently available.","affiliation":"Univ Rouen Normandie, Dynamicure INSERM UMR 1311, CHU Rouen, Department of Paediatrics and adolescent Medicine, F-76000, Rouen, France.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Phenotypes","eosinophils","Endotypes","T2 Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.resmer.2026.101321"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-11","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-12","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42938296","source":"MED","pmid":"42938296","pmcid":"PMC13704197","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13704197"]},"doi":"10.3390/genes17101182","title":"The Role of Non-Coding RNAs in the Pathogenesis of Asthma.","authorString":"Birger R, Plewa P, Komaniecka N, Bakinowska E, Graczyk P, Pawlik A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Birger R","lastName":"Birger","firstName":"Radosław","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6256-5695"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental and Clinical Pharmacology, Pomeranian Medical University, 70-111 Szczecin, Poland."},{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Pharmacodynamics, Pomeranian Medical University, 71-244 Szczecin, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Plewa P","lastName":"Plewa","firstName":"Paulina","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6821-0086"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Pomeranian Medical University, 70-111 Szczecin, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Komaniecka N","lastName":"Komaniecka","firstName":"Nina","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-3162-4870"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental and Clinical Pharmacology, Pomeranian Medical University, 70-111 Szczecin, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Bakinowska E","lastName":"Bakinowska","firstName":"Estera","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Pomeranian Medical University, 70-111 Szczecin, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Graczyk P","lastName":"Graczyk","firstName":"Patryk","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-8963-6882"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University Clinical Hospital in Poznan, 60-355 Poznan, Poland."}]}},{"fullName":"Pawlik A","lastName":"Pawlik","firstName":"Andrzej","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6557-1208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology, Pomeranian Medical University, 70-111 Szczecin, Poland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6557-1208"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-3162-4870"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-8963-6882"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6256-5695"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6821-0086"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4299057,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Genes (Basel)","title":"Genes","essn":"2073-4425","issn":"2073-4425","nlmid":"101551097","isoabbreviation":"Genes (Basel)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1182","abstractText":"Asthma is a chronic inflammatory airway disease characterized by considerable clinical and molecular heterogeneity, involving complex interactions between genetic susceptibility, environmental factors, and immune dysregulation. Increasing evidence indicates that non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs), including microRNAs (miRNAs), long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs), circular RNAs (circRNAs), and other regulatory RNA species, play essential roles in the development and progression of asthma-modulate inflammatory responses, immune cell differentiation, airway remodeling, and epithelial barrier function by interacting with key molecular and signaling pathways, notably the NF-κB, JAK/STAT, PI3K/AKT/mTOR, and TGF-β/Smad cascades. For example, miR-125b-5p and miR-223-3p demonstrate consistent dysregulation across varying biological compartments and independent cohorts, while miR-21, miR-146a, miR-155, and lncRNAs like NEAT1 and MALAT1 are frequently confirmed as central regulators of airway pathology. Aberrant expression of these ncRNAs has been associated with distinct asthma phenotypes, disease severity, and therapeutic responses, highlighting their potential as diagnostic and prognostic biomarkers. This review summarizes current knowledge on the biological functions of ncRNAs in asthma, their involvement in critical pathogenic mechanisms, and discusses their emerging clinical applications and the challenges of translating RNA-based therapeutics into clinical practice.","affiliation":"Department of Experimental and Clinical Pharmacology, Pomeranian Medical University, 70-111 Szczecin, Poland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"MicroRNAs","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"RNA, Untranslated","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Signal Transduction","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Airway Remodeling","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"RNA, Long Noncoding","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biomarkers","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Circular","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Biomarkers","Airway inflammation","airway remodeling","microRNAs","Non-coding Rnas","Long Non-coding Rnas","Circular Rnas"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA, circular","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"RNA, Untranslated","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"MicroRNAs","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"RNA, Long Noncoding","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Biomarkers","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/genes17101182"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13704197"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13704197?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["geo","gen","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-27","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42882219","source":"MED","pmid":"42882219","doi":"10.3390/jcm15197761","title":"Cognitive Impairment in Asthma: Emerging Evidence, Clinical Consequences, and Future Directions.","authorString":"Mengash A, Siraj RA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mengash A","lastName":"Mengash","firstName":"Aminah","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-6700-8105"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Therapy, College of Applied Medical Sciences, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa 31982, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Siraj RA","lastName":"Siraj","firstName":"Rayan A","initials":"RA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4238-9419"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Therapy, College of Applied Medical Sciences, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa 31982, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4238-9419"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-6700-8105"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"19","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4289908,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","title":"Journal of clinical medicine","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383","nlmid":"101606588","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"7761","abstractText":"Asthma is increasingly recognised as a systemic inflammatory disease with effects extending beyond the respiratory system. Emerging evidence suggests that cognitive impairment may represent an under-recognised extrapulmonary manifestation with important implications for disease management and long-term neurological health. This narrative review critically synthesises current evidence regarding the epidemiology, clinical consequences, and potential mechanisms linking asthma with cognitive dysfunction. Observational and longitudinal studies indicate that asthma is associated with an increased risk of cognitive impairment, cognitive decline, and dementia. However, substantial heterogeneity in study design, cognitive assessment, asthma phenotyping, and adjustment for confounding factors limits causal inference. Cognitive deficits predominantly involve attention, executive function, memory, and, less consistently, processing speed, potentially compromising medication adherence, inhaler technique, symptom perception, and self-management. Current evidence supports a multifactorial pathophysiological model in which chronic systemic inflammation, oxidative stress, intermittent hypoxaemia, sleep disturbance, corticosteroid exposure, and psychological comorbidity interact to promote endothelial dysfunction, blood-brain barrier disruption, microglial activation, neuroinflammation, mitochondrial dysfunction, and impaired synaptic plasticity. These mechanisms may be particularly relevant in children with early-onset asthma, older adults, and patients with severe or poorly controlled disease, although direct mechanistic evidence remains limited. Future research should prioritise well-phenotyped longitudinal cohorts that integrate comprehensive cognitive assessment with neuroimaging, inflammatory biomarkers, and objective measures of disease control to establish temporal relationships, identify high-risk populations, and determine whether cognitive dysfunction is a modifiable component of asthma burden. Recognition of cognition as a clinically relevant outcome may ultimately improve risk stratification and support more personalised, holistic approaches to asthma care.","affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Therapy, College of Applied Medical Sciences, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa 31982, Saudi Arabia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Cognitive impairment","neuroinflammation","Cognitive Decline"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15197761"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-20","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42957048","source":"MED","pmid":"42957048","doi":"10.1016/j.jaip.2026.10.024","title":"Increased Exacerbation Reduction Over Time with Tezepelumab in Severe Asthma: CROSSROADS-4.","authorString":"DeMartino JK, Ambrose CS, Xiang C, Mu F, Gao Y, Ho A, Chen J, Queen DA, Sanchez SZ, Soong W, Tan L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"DeMartino JK","lastName":"DeMartino","firstName":"Jessica K","initials":"JK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AstraZeneca, Wilmington, DE, USA. Electronic address: jessica.demartino@astrazeneca.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Ambrose CS","lastName":"Ambrose","firstName":"Christopher S","initials":"CS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AstraZeneca, Wilmington, DE, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Xiang C","lastName":"Xiang","firstName":"Cheryl","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Analysis Group, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Mu F","lastName":"Mu","firstName":"Fan","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Analysis Group, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gao Y","lastName":"Gao","firstName":"Yipeng","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5917-1005"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Analysis Group, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ho A","lastName":"Ho","firstName":"Allister","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Analysis Group, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen J","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Jingyi","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Analysis Group, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Queen DA","lastName":"Queen","firstName":"David A","initials":"DA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AstraZeneca, Wilmington, DE, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sanchez SZ","lastName":"Sanchez","firstName":"Santiago Zuluaga","initials":"SZ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Amgen, Uxbridge, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Soong W","lastName":"Soong","firstName":"Weily","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AllerVie Health - Alabama Allergy & Asthma Center, Birmingham, AL, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Tan L","lastName":"Tan","firstName":"Laren","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Loma Linda University Health, Loma Linda, CA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5917-1005"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4276179,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract","title":"The journal of allergy and clinical immunology. In practice","essn":"2213-2201","issn":"2213-2198","nlmid":"101597220","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S2213-2198(26)00902-5","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Tezepelumab is a monoclonal antibody approved in the United States for severe asthma. Real-world evidence of effectiveness remains limited.<h4>Objective</h4>To evaluate the effectiveness of tezepelumab in reducing annualized asthma exacerbation rates (AAERs) and healthcare resource utilization (HRU) in the United States.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective, self-controlled, pre-post cohort study used data from Symphony Health, an ICON plc Company, PatientSource®, December 1, 2020, to June 30, 2024. Eligible patients were ≥12 years, had ≥2 records of tezepelumab, and experienced ≥2 asthma exacerbations in the 12 months before tezepelumab initiation, with index date being the day after initiation. AAERs and asthma exacerbation-related HRU were compared between the 12 months pre- and post-index. AAERs were also evaluated across patient subgroups.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 527 patients were included. AAERs decreased significantly from 2.81 to 1.26 per person-year (55% decrease; 95% confidence interval [CI] [51%, 59%]; p<0.001). The greatest benefit was observed among patients starting treatment February 2023 and later (61% decrease; [53%-67%]; p<0.001). Consistent reductions were seen across subgroups: patients with pre-index blood eosinophil count <300 cells/μL and ≥300 cells/μL, pre-index total serum immunoglobulin E levels <100 IU/mL and ≥100 IU/mL, biologic-naïve and biologic-experienced, persistent tezepelumab users, and those with comorbid chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyposis. Asthma exacerbation-related HRU declined significantly (p<0.001) with 50-54% reductions across healthcare settings.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In this real-world study, patients with severe asthma treated with tezepelumab had substantial reductions in asthma exacerbations and HRU, with the greatest benefits among more recent initiators.","affiliation":"AstraZeneca, Wilmington, DE, USA. Electronic address: jessica.demartino@astrazeneca.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Amgen Inc","orderIn":0},{"agency":"AstraZeneca PLC","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Exacerbation","Healthcare Resource Utilization","Real-world Study","Tezepelumab"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaip.2026.10.024"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42562345","source":"MED","pmid":"42562345","doi":"10.1016/j.jaip.2026.07.036","title":"Development of the Six-Item Long-Term Asthma Control Questionnaire: a Questionnaire for Assessing Long-Term Asthma Control.","authorString":"Kurosawa Y, Gon Y, Ukiya E, Kawamura M, Ozoe R, Yamada S, Kozu Y, Oki T, Shimizu T, Iikura M, Maruoka S, Mizumura K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kurosawa Y","lastName":"Kurosawa","firstName":"Yusuke","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Gon Y","lastName":"Gon","firstName":"Yasuhiro","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ukiya E","lastName":"Ukiya","firstName":"Eiko","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kawamura M","lastName":"Kawamura","firstName":"Michio","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ozoe R","lastName":"Ozoe","firstName":"Ryosuke","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamada S","lastName":"Yamada","firstName":"Shiho","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kozu Y","lastName":"Kozu","firstName":"Yutaka","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Oki T","lastName":"Oki","firstName":"Takashi","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Shimizu T","lastName":"Shimizu","firstName":"Tetsuo","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Iikura M","lastName":"Iikura","firstName":"Motoyasu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Maruoka S","lastName":"Maruoka","firstName":"Shuichiro","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Mizumura K","lastName":"Mizumura","firstName":"Kenji","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan. Electronic address: mizumura.kenji@nihon-u.ac.jp."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4309346,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract","title":"The journal of allergy and clinical immunology. In practice","essn":"2213-2201","issn":"2213-2198","nlmid":"101597220","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol Pract"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2696-2704.e6","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Existing asthma questionnaires rely on short recall periods and may not capture symptom burden across typical follow-up intervals. A brief instrument that summarizes longer-term asthma control may support risk stratification and remission-oriented care.<h4>Objective</h4>To develop a brief questionnaire that assesses symptom burden over a 3-month recall period and evaluate its performance in identifying Asthma Control Test (ACT)-defined, well-controlled asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>Ninety adults with asthma completed a questionnaire, the six-item Long-Term Asthma Control Questionnaire (LAQ-6), and the ACT in an outpatient setting. We performed correlation and receiver operating characteristic curve analyses to evaluate associations with ACT-defined well-controlled status (ACT ≥20). We assessed confirmed exacerbations and emergency visits over an approximately 6-month observation window using receiver operating characteristic curve analyses and prespecified cutoffs.<h4>Results</h4>The LAQ-6 strongly correlated with the ACT (r = 0.79; P < .001) and asthma-related quality of life assessed using mean Asthma Quality of Life Questionnaire scores (Spearman's ρ = 0.763; P < .001). It achieved an area under the curve of 0.83 in identifying ACT-defined well-controlled status (ACT ≥20) (95% CI, 0.72-0.94). For 6-month outcomes, the LAQ-6 achieved higher areas under the curve than the ACT for exacerbations (0.866 vs 0.816) and emergency visits (0.857 vs 0.676). Exacerbations and emergency visits were more frequent in patients with LAQ-6 scores below the prespecified cutoff (<27).<h4>Conclusions</h4>The LAQ-6 is a brief 3-month recall questionnaire complementing existing short-recall tools. It may facilitate rapid assessment of longer-term symptom burden and risk stratification in outpatient practice, supporting evaluation of clinical remission.","affiliation":"Division of Respiratory Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine, Nihon University School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"e