"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9501-8606"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Harvard Medical School, Boston, Mass; Division of Immunology, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, Mass; Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care, Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, Mass; Department of Environmental Health, Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, Boston, Mass. Electronic address: PLAI@mgh.harvard.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9501-8606"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"158","journalIssueId":4308638,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol","title":"The Journal of allergy and clinical immunology","essn":"1097-6825","issn":"0091-6749","nlmid":"1275002","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1165-1177","abstractText":"The field of asthma microbiome research has shifted rapidly in recent years. Advances in sequencing technology have led to an increased ability to characterize multikingdom microbial species and integration with host omics profiling to enhance future translational applications. Traditional bacteria-centric, cross-sectional studies are giving way to mechanistic frameworks that incorporate fungi, viruses, and host-immune interactions. In this state-of-the-art review of emerging concepts in microbiome asthma research, we first propose a structured framework to consider microbiome studies across 5 major domains-microbial kingdom, site of sampling, integration with host omics, clinical outcome domain, and translational relevance-in order to synthesize recent high-impact human microbiome studies in asthma. We highlight emerging evidence that fungal and viral communities contribute independently to asthma risk and that human microbial communities are linked to distinct inflammatory and immune pathways shaped by host genetic susceptibility.","affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary Medicine, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, Mass; Harvard Medical School, Boston, Mass.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Asthma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Microbiota","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Multiomics"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Genomics","Asthma","Microbiome"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaci.2026.08.026"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-17","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-04","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-18","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42940542","source":"MED","pmid":"42940542","pmcid":"PMC13704452","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13704452"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14203497","title":"Assessing the Need and Acceptance of a Smart Asthma Management Application Among Patients in Saudi Arabia.","authorString":"Kolhar M, Aldabayan YS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kolhar M","lastName":"Kolhar","firstName":"Manjur","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9152-3242"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Information Management and Technology, College of Applied Medical Sciences, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa 31982, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Aldabayan YS","lastName":"Aldabayan","firstName":"Yousef Saad","initials":"YS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9498-946X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Respiratory Care, College of Applied Medical Sciences, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa 31982, Saudi Arabia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9152-3242"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9498-946X"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"20","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4299046,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032","nlmid":"101666525","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"3497","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Digital health technologies can support asthma self-management through symptom tracking, medication reminders, environmental alerts, and communication with healthcare providers. However, evidence regarding patients' perceived needs and acceptance of an integrated smart asthma management application in Saudi Arabia remains limited.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to assess perceived need for and intended acceptance of a Saudi-based smart asthma management application among adults with asthma and identify factors associated with their likelihood of using the proposed application.<h4>Methods</h4>A cross-sectional questionnaire-based study was conducted among adults with asthma in Saudi Arabia. The questionnaire assessed asthma self-management experiences, environmental challenges, previous digital health application use, and preferences for proposed application features. Descriptive statistics, Spearman's rank correlations, and ordinal logistic regression were performed. Internal consistency of the five core feature-needs items was evaluated using Cronbach's alpha. The Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate procedure was applied to adjust for multiple correlation comparisons.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 68 adults were included in the final analysis. Overall, 77.9% reported a moderate-to-high need for the proposed application, 58.8% expressed moderate-to-high willingness to use it, and 66.2% indicated that they were likely or very likely to use a Saudi-based smart asthma application. The five core feature-needs items demonstrated high internal consistency (Cronbach's α = 0.899). Overall perceived need showed the strongest positive correlation with likelihood of use (ρ = 0.709, 95% CI: 0.545-0.844, FDR-adjusted <i>q</i> < 0.001). Significant positive correlations were also observed for environmental alerts (ρ = 0.683), Sehhaty integration (ρ = 0.674), core feature-needs score (ρ = 0.667), perceived environmental burden (ρ = 0.646), and smartwatch connectivity (ρ = 0.590), all <i>q</i> < 0.001. In the adjusted ordinal logistic regression model, perceived environmental burden (aOR = 4.09, 95% CI: 2.09-7.98, <i>p</i> < 0.001) and need for additional asthma-management support (aOR = 3.17, 95% CI: 1.62-6.21, <i>p</i> < 0.001) were independently associated with greater likelihood of intended application use.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Adults with asthma demonstrated substantial perceived need for and willingness to use a Saudi-based smart asthma management application. Perceived environmental burden and need for additional asthma-management support were particularly associated with intended use. These findings support user-centered development and prospective evaluation of an integrated application incorporating contextually relevant asthma self-management and digital health features.","affiliation":"Department of Health Information Management and Technology, College of Applied Medical Sciences, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa 31982, Saudi Arabia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Asthma","Environmental exposure","Saudi Arabia","Self-management","Mobile Applications","Mobile Health","Digital Health"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14203497"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13704452"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13704452?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-27","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42968994","source":"MED","pmid":"42968994","pmcid":"PMC13724090","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13724090"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.117356","title":"Asthma Control in Children Followed in a Tertiary Pediatric Pulmonology Clinic: A Real-World Observational Study.","authorString":"Toliopoulos F, Messaritakis K, Bizakis M, Giachoudis A, Karazafeiris N, Karazafeiris S, Sofianopoulos G, Vakouftsi EA, Papadopoulou-Legbelou K, Kampouras A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Toliopoulos F","lastName":"Toliopoulos","firstName":"Filippos","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Messaritakis K","lastName":"Messaritakis","firstName":"Konstantinos","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Bizakis M","lastName":"Bizakis","firstName":"Michail","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Giachoudis A","lastName":"Giachoudis","firstName":"Alexios","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Karazafeiris N","lastName":"Karazafeiris","firstName":"Nikolaos","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Karazafeiris S","lastName":"Karazafeiris","firstName":"Stergios","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, University of Thessaly, Larissa, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Sofianopoulos G","lastName":"Sofianopoulos","firstName":"George","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Vakouftsi EA","lastName":"Vakouftsi","firstName":"Elisavet A","initials":"EA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"4th Department of Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Papadopoulou-Legbelou K","lastName":"Papadopoulou-Legbelou","firstName":"Kyriaki","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"4th Department of Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}},{"fullName":"Kampouras A","lastName":"Kampouras","firstName":"Asterios","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"4th Department of Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4276710,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","title":"Cureus","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184","nlmid":"101596737","isoabbreviation":"Cureus"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e117356","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Achieving adequate asthma control remains a major challenge in pediatric patients, even among those followed in specialist clinics. Real-world data describing asthma control in routine clinical practice are limited.<h4>Objective</h4>To describe asthma control assessed by the Asthma Control Test (ACT) in children followed in a tertiary pediatric pulmonology clinic and, as exploratory secondary aims, to describe the distribution of age, exacerbation frequency, controller treatment step, and total immunoglobulin E (IgE) in relation to asthma control.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective, descriptive observational study included children with physician-diagnosed asthma followed in a tertiary pediatric pulmonology clinic between January 2023 and December 2024. Asthma control was assessed with the standard ACT, administered to all children, and defined as controlled (ACT ≥20) or uncontrolled (ACT <20). Variables included age, number of exacerbations during the previous year, controller treatment step according to the Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA), and total IgE. Continuous variables were summarized as median and interquartile range (IQR). Group comparisons were performed with the Mann-Whitney U and Fisher's exact tests, correlations with Spearman's rank correlation, and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were reported for all estimates.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 36 children were included; an ACT score was available for 34 (94%), who formed the analysed sample. Twenty children (58.8%) had controlled asthma, and 14 (41.2%, 95% CI 26.4% to 57.8%) had uncontrolled asthma. Children with uncontrolled asthma tended to be older than those with controlled asthma (median age 8.5 vs 6.5 years; median difference 2.0 years, 95% CI -0.5 to 5.0), although this difference did not reach statistical significance (p = 0.052). Step 3 or higher controller therapy was recorded in 8 of 14 children with uncontrolled asthma (57.1%) and 8 of 20 with controlled asthma (40.0%; p = 0.49). In an exploratory analysis, ACT score was inversely correlated with controller treatment step (Spearman ρ = -0.36, 95% CI -0.62 to -0.02; p = 0.04), consistent with treatment escalation in children with more difficult asthma. Exploratory correlation analyses showed a moderate negative correlation between ACT score and total IgE (Spearman ρ = -0.42, 95% CI -0.79 to 0.17; p = 0.15; n = 13) and a weak negative correlation between ACT score and exacerbation frequency (ρ = -0.29, 95% CI -0.57 to 0.05; p = 0.09; n = 34), neither of which reached statistical significance.<h4>Conclusions</h4>A substantial proportion of children followed in a tertiary pediatric pulmonology clinic had suboptimal asthma control despite specialist care. Older children tended to have poorer asthma control, although this difference did not reach statistical significance, and lower ACT scores were associated with higher-intensity controller therapy, most plausibly reflecting treatment escalation in children with more difficult asthma rather than a treatment effect. These exploratory findings highlight the need for age-appropriate asthma management strategies in real-world practice.","affiliation":"Pediatrics, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, Thessaloniki, GRC.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Children","Asthma","Asthma Control Test","Pediatric Pulmonology","Real-world Study"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.117356"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13724090"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13724090?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-11-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-11-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42857083","source":"MED","pmid":"42857083","doi":"10.3389/fped.2026.1949534","title":"Family-centered nursing interventions on asthma control and caregiver management capacity in preschool children with asthma: a systematic review and meta-analysis.","authorString":"Pan Y, Ye X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan Y","lastName":"Pan","firstName":"Yiling","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, Hangzhou Children's Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye X","lastName":"Ye","firstName":"Xuefen","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gastroenterology, Hangzhou Children's Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4112092,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Pediatr","title":"Frontiers in pediatrics","essn":"2296-2360","issn":"2296-2360","nlmid":"101615492","isoabbreviation":"Front Pediatr"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1949534","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Systematic evaluation of the impact of family-centered nursing interventions on asthma control and caregiver management abilities in preschool children with asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) published from database inception to December 2025 were searched in PubMed, Embase, Web of Science, CINAHL, Scopus, and Chinese databases. Literature screening and data extraction were independently completed by two researchers, and the quality was evaluated by the Cochrane risk-of-bias tool for randomized trials (RoB 2.0). Meta-analysis was performed using RevMan 5.4 software.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 15 RCTs involving 457 children were included. The meta-analysis results showed that, compared with routine care, family-centered nursing interventions significantly improved the Asthma Control Test score (MD = 2.49, 95% CI: 1.15-3.83), quality of life score (SMD = 1.02, 95% CI: 0.52-1.51), and lung function indicators (MD = 6.53, 95% CI: 1.03-12.03). Furthermore, family-centered nursing interventions significantly increased the symptom control rate (3 studies, RR = 5.24, 95% CI: 3.14-8.73) and caregiver knowledge mastery rate (3 studies, RR = 2.36, 95% CI: 1.30-4.29), while reducing the rehospitalization rate (3 studies, RR = 0.24, 95% CI: 0.11-0.55).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Family-centered nursing intervention can effectively improve outcomes associated with clinical symptoms, lung function and quality of life in preschool children with asthma, enhance caregivers' asthma management capacity and reduce the use of medical resources, which is worthy of popularization and application in clinical practice.<h4>Systematic review registration</h4>https://inplasy.com/?s=INPLASY202670039, identifier INPLASY202670039.","affiliation":"Department of Nursing, Hangzhou Children's Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Children","Asthma","Meta-analysis","Preschool children","Systematic review","Randomized controlled trial","Disease Management","Family-centered Nursing"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fped.2026.1949534"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42863803","source":"MED","pmid":"42863803","doi":"10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109214","title":"Association between obesity and asthma among Arab Israeli adolescents compared with Jewish Israeli peers.","authorString":"Treister-Goltzman Y, Menashe I, Nemet D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Treister-Goltzman Y","lastName":"Treister-Goltzman","firstName":"Yulia","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine and Siaal Research Center for Family Practice and Primary Care, The Haim Doron Division of Community Health, Faculty of Health Sciences, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer-Sheva, Israel; Clalit Health Services, Southern district, Israel. Electronic address: yuliatr@walla.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Menashe I","lastName":"Menashe","firstName":"Idan","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Epidemiology, Biostatistics, and Community Health Sciences, Faculty of Health Sciences, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer-Sheva, Israel. Electronic address: idanmen@bgu.ac.il."}]}},{"fullName":"Nemet D","lastName":"Nemet","firstName":"Dan","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3289-6425"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Child Health and Sports Center, Meir Medical Center, Tel Aviv University School of Medicine, Tel Aviv, Israel. Electronic address: dan.nemet@clalit.org.il."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3289-6425"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"264","journalIssueId":4294503,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109214","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Ethnic differences in the health implications of adolescent obesity, including asthma, are understudied in Israel. We compared associations between adolescent weight categories and asthma among Arab Israeli and Jewish Israeli adolescents.<h4>Methods</h4>This retrospective cross-sectional study used the computerized database of Clalit Health Services (CHS). It included Arab Israeli adolescents aged 14-19 years with at least one recorded height and weight measurement between 2007 and 2022. A Jewish Israeli comparison group meeting the same eligibility criteria was selected, with a socioeconomic status distribution corresponding to that of Arab Israeli adolescents. The final sample included 313,936 Arab Israeli and 289,616 Jewish Israeli adolescents. Logistic regression assessed associations between weight categories and asthma overall and asthma with inhaled corticosteroid (ICS), inhaled bronchodilator (IBD), or systemic corticosteroid (SCS) use, separately by group.<h4>Results</h4>Higher weight categories were associated with higher odds of asthma, with similar patterns in both groups. Among adolescents with class 3 obesity, adjusted odds ratios (95% CI) were 1.52 (1.35-1.70) for asthma overall, 2.02 (1.23-3.10) for asthma with ICS use, 2.81 (1.49-4.78) for asthma with IBD use, and 3.81 (2.16-6.19) for asthma with SCS use. Jewish Israeli adolescents had higher ICS use, whereas Arab Israeli adolescents had higher SCS use.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Asthma management should address obesity through healthy diet and physical activity while considering lifestyle differences between Arab Israeli and Jewish Israeli adolescents. Greater ICS use and reduced SCS use among Arab Israeli adolescents may warrant attention.","affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine and Siaal Research Center for Family Practice and Primary Care, The Haim Doron Division of Community Health, Faculty of Health Sciences, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer-Sheva, Israel; Clalit Health Services, Southern district, Israel. Electronic address: yuliatr@walla.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Sex","Asthma","Ethnicity","Adolescent Obesity","Weight Categories","Israeli Subpopulations"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109214"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42918263","source":"MED","pmid":"42918263","doi":"10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109234","title":"Equity in asthma research: A meta-analysis on participant diversity in U.S. clinical trials.","authorString":"Nguyen K, Hazlitt R, Jones G, Hemmerich C, Howard H, Babek JT, Schoutko A, Anderson B, Ford AI, Vassar M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nguyen K","lastName":"Nguyen","firstName":"Khanh","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA. Electronic address: khanh.nguyen11@okstate.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Hazlitt R","lastName":"Hazlitt","firstName":"Rachel","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Jones G","lastName":"Jones","firstName":"Garrett","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hemmerich C","lastName":"Hemmerich","firstName":"Christian","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Howard H","lastName":"Howard","firstName":"Haley","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1142-8104"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Babek JT","lastName":"Babek","firstName":"J Tyler","initials":"JT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4212-3792"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Schoutko A","lastName":"Schoutko","firstName":"Abigail","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, Oklahoma State University Medical Center, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Anderson B","lastName":"Anderson","firstName":"Bradley","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, Oklahoma State University Medical Center, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ford AI","lastName":"Ford","firstName":"Alicia Ito","initials":"AI","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6866-2639"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA; Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Vassar M","lastName":"Vassar","firstName":"Matt","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA; Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1142-8104"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4212-3792"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6866-2639"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"264","journalIssueId":4294503,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Respir Med","title":"Respiratory medicine","essn":"1532-3064","issn":"0954-6111","nlmid":"8908438","isoabbreviation":"Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109234","abstractText":"<h4>Rationale</h4>The increasing diagnosis of asthma in the United States has disproportionately affected historically marginalized groups, leading to higher healthcare costs and more missed days due to illness. Despite ongoing improvements, there is a significant gap in comprehensive diversity analyses within current clinical trials for asthma.<h4>Objectives</h4>To assess the degree of relevant demographic reporting in current clinical trials concerning asthma.<h4>Methods</h4>We performed a systematic review and meta-analysis on the reported sex, age, and race/ethnicity of participants in all asthma-related clinical trials in the U.S. published from January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2023. We extracted all clinical trials for sex, age, and race. A participation-to-disease representation ratio (PDRR) was determined for each demographic and qualitative assessment was determined from the summation of the score.<h4>Measurements and main results</h4>Of the initial 1757 search returns, 116 were eligible for full-screen extraction based on our criteria. In regard to sex, 39 trials (39/116, 33.6%) have an overall representation rating of Good. Only 1 trial (1/116, 0.8%) was given a rating of Good for overall race/ethnicity representation. Age group data was rarely reported, with only 2 trials (2/116, 1.7%) reporting inclusion of older adults.<h4>Conclusions</h4>While current U.S.-based asthma clinical trials are making noticeable progress in sex/gender inclusivity, there are still significant shortcomings in the inclusion and reporting of non-white racial and ethnic groups. It is imperative that future asthma clinical trials address this limitation to ensure equitable treatment for all populations.","affiliation":"Office of Medical Student Research, Oklahoma State University Center for Health Sciences, Tulsa, OK, USA. Electronic address: khanh.nguyen11@okstate.edu.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Health Disparities","Chronic Airflow Obstruction","Bronchial Disease","Asthma Epidemiology"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rmed.2026.109234"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42887703","source":"MED","pmid":"42887703","doi":"10.1016/j.jaci.2026.09.028","title":"Lower Nasal Sphingolipids in Pediatric Asthma and Cell-Type-Specific Effects of 17q21 Variants.","authorString":"Solomon Z, Gomi R, Verzani Z, Wu A, Heras A, Permaul P, Balaji U, Gu J, Gomi K, Brown S, de Huerta NA, Gosnell N, Christos P, Ono JG, Elemento O, Worgall TS, Worgall S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Solomon Z","lastName":"Solomon","firstName":"Zenna","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Gomi R","lastName":"Gomi","firstName":"Rika","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Verzani Z","lastName":"Verzani","firstName":"Zoe","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Population Health Sciences, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu A","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Alan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Population Health Sciences, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Heras A","lastName":"Heras","firstName":"Andrea","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY; Drukier Institute for Children's Health, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Permaul P","lastName":"Permaul","firstName":"Perdita","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY; Drukier Institute for Children's Health, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Balaji U","lastName":"Balaji","firstName":"Uthra","initials":"U","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Drukier Institute for Children's Health, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Gu J","lastName":"Gu","firstName":"Jinghua","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Drukier Institute for Children's Health, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Gomi K","lastName":"Gomi","firstName":"Kazunori","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Brown S","lastName":"Brown","firstName":"Sarah","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"de Huerta NA","lastName":"de Huerta","firstName":"Natalia Amos","initials":"NA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Gosnell N","lastName":"Gosnell","firstName":"Natalie","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Christos P","lastName":"Christos","firstName":"Paul","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Population Health Sciences, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Ono JG","lastName":"Ono","firstName":"Jennie G","initials":"JG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY; Drukier Institute for Children's Health, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Elemento O","lastName":"Elemento","firstName":"Olivier","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Caryl and Israel Englander Institute for Precision Medicine, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Worgall TS","lastName":"Worgall","firstName":"Tilla S","initials":"TS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology & Cell Biology, Columbia University New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Worgall S","lastName":"Worgall","firstName":"Stefan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY; Drukier Institute for Children's Health, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY; Department of Genetic Medicine, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY. Electronic address: stw2006@med.cornell.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4275613,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol","title":"The Journal of allergy and clinical immunology","essn":"1097-6825","issn":"0091-6749","nlmid":"1275002","isoabbreviation":"J Allergy Clin Immunol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S0091-6749(26)00723-2","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Genetic variations at the 17q21 asthma-risk locus regulate GSDMB and ORMDL3, influencing inflammatory pathways and sphingolipid metabolism. Although 17q21 variants such as rs7216389 and rs12936231 are associated with ORMDL3 expression in immune and airway smooth muscle cells, their effects on airway epithelial sphingolipids remain unclear.<h4>Objectives</h4>To determine whether asthma or 17q21 asthma genotypes alter sphingolipid composition in the upper respiratory tract in children, and to define contributions of epithelial versus immune cells.<h4>Methods</h4>Sphingolipids were quantified by mass spectrometry in nasal fluid and plasma from children with and without asthma. Nasal epithelial cells and PBMCs from healthy adults homozygous for rs7216389 C/C or T/T (risk) were analyzed for sphingolipid levels and ORMDL3/GSDMB associated gene expression.<h4>Results</h4>Children with atopic asthma had significantly lower nasal dihydroceramides, ceramides, and sphingosine independent of inhaled corticosteroids and allergic rhinitis. Asthma was associated with increased plasma sphingolipids but decreased sphingolipids in blood cells, mirroring nasal findings. In PBMCs, the rs7216389 T allele was associated with increased ORMDL3 expression and suppressed de-novo sphingolipid synthesis. In nasal epithelial cells, the T allele predominantly increased GSDMB expression and did not alter sphingolipid metabolism.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Nasal mucosal sphingolipids are reduced in children with atopic asthma and sphingolipids generated by de-novo sphingolipid synthesis may serve as a noninvasive biomarker of disease. 17q21 risk alleles can exert cell-type-specific effects: ORMDL3-mediated suppression of sphingolipid synthesis occurs in immune cells but not in airway epithelial cells. Systemic immune or inflammatory cell-driven alterations may contribute to reduced airway sphingolipids in asthma.","affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Weill Cornell Medicine New York, NY.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Genetics","Nasal mucosa","Sphingolipids","Childhood Asthma"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaci.2026.09.028"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-16","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42948851","source":"MED","pmid":"42948851","pmcid":"PMC13713060","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13713060"]},"doi":"10.1097/ee9.0000000000000544","title":"Associations between air pollution and pediatric asthma hospitalizations across vulnerable groups in Louisiana.","authorString":"Luglio DG, Hossain CA, Monis RP, Ramesh B, Proven E, Rahman MM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Luglio DG","lastName":"Luglio","firstName":"David G","initials":"DG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4669-547X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Environmental Health Sciences, Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, Tulane University, New Orleans, Louisiana."}]}},{"fullName":"Hossain CA","lastName":"Hossain","firstName":"Chowdhury A","initials":"CA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Environmental Health Sciences, Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, Tulane University, New Orleans, Louisiana."}]}},{"fullName":"Monis RP","lastName":"Monis","firstName":"Rica P","initials":"RP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Environmental Health Sciences, Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, Tulane University, New Orleans, Louisiana."}]}},{"fullName":"Ramesh B","lastName":"Ramesh","firstName":"Balaji","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Environmental Health Sciences, Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, Tulane University, New Orleans, Louisiana."}]}},{"fullName":"Proven E","lastName":"Proven","firstName":"Erin","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Louisiana Department of Health, Bureau of Health Informatics, Office of Public Health, Baton Rouge, Louisiana."}]}},{"fullName":"Rahman MM","lastName":"Rahman","firstName":"Md Mostafijur","initials":"MM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Environmental Health Sciences, Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, Tulane University, New Orleans, Louisiana."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4669-547X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"10","journalIssueId":4294652,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Environ Epidemiol","title":"Environmental epidemiology (Philadelphia, Pa.)","essn":"2474-7882","issn":"2474-7882","nlmid":"101719527","isoabbreviation":"Environ Epidemiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-12-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e544","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Different socioeconomic and racial groups experience different environmental and health burdens across the United States, including for asthma. Understanding the disparate health effects of air pollution on asthma in Louisiana, a particularly vulnerable region, is limited.<h4>Objective</h4>To evaluate whether associations of air pollution with pediatric asthma exacerbation vary across vulnerable groups in Louisiana.<h4>Methods</h4>We analyzed 28,499 asthma hospitalization cases for <18-year-old individuals across Louisiana from 2010 to 2019 using a time-stratified case-crossover design, with a distributed lag nonlinear model up to 3-lag days. Conditional logistic regression was used to estimate odds ratios (OR) for associations between PM<sub>2.5</sub>, O<sub>3</sub>, and NO<sub>2</sub> exposures and asthma hospitalizations. NO<sub>2</sub> data were limited to 2010-2016. Race and socioeconomic (by Medicaid status) effect modification were examined.<h4>Results</h4>Cumulative exposures across lags 0-3 days were significantly associated with increased odds of asthma hospitalization for PM<sub>2.5</sub> (OR = 1.079; 95% confidence interval = [1.052, 1.107]), O<sub>3</sub> (1.082 [1.050, 1.116]), and NO<sub>2</sub> (1.071 [1.034, 1.111]). Race-effect modification was not significant. Despite this, significant lag-specific patterns differed by race, with associations observed at lags 1-2 among Black children and at lag 3 among White children. In multipollutant models, associations were attenuated for O<sub>3</sub> and NO<sub>2</sub>, while PM<sub>2.5</sub> remained positively associated with asthma hospitalizations.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Short-term increases in air pollution exposures are associated with an increased risk of hospitalizations for asthma among young populations in Louisiana. Given cumulative associations were similar across subgroups, the higher burden among Black children potentially highlights persistent exposure disparities. Reducing air pollution exposure may improve pediatric respiratory health.","affiliation":"Department of Environmental Health Sciences, Celia Scott Weatherhead School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine, Tulane University, New Orleans, Louisiana.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Air pollution","Health Disparities","Racial Disparities","Hospitalizations","Pediatric Asthma","Socio-Economic Vulnerabilities"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/EE9.0000000000000544"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13713060"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13713060?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42910584","source":"MED","pmid":"42910584","pmcid":"PMC13722450","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13722450"]},"doi":"10.5826/mrm.2026.1142","title":"Tracking severe asthma improvement with biologic therapy using spirometry and impulse oscillometry: a real-world study.","authorString":"Chiei Gallo A, Ventura L, Caron L, Guglieri M, Bono E, Iemoli E, Carpagnano GE, Portacci A, Tursi F, Ortolani V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chiei Gallo A","lastName":"Chiei Gallo","firstName":"Alessandra","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8028-5453"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Ventura L","lastName":"Ventura","firstName":"Laura","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Statistical Sciences, University of Padua, Padua (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Caron L","lastName":"Caron","firstName":"Lea","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Guglieri M","lastName":"Guglieri","firstName":"Marta","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Bono E","lastName":"Bono","firstName":"Eleonora","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Iemoli E","lastName":"Iemoli","firstName":"Enrico","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Carpagnano GE","lastName":"Carpagnano","firstName":"Giovanna Elisiana","initials":"GE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Respiratory Disease, Department of Translational Biomedicine and neuroscience, University \"Aldo Moro\", Bari (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Portacci A","lastName":"Portacci","firstName":"Andrea","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Respiratory Disease, Department of Translational Biomedicine and neuroscience, University \"Aldo Moro\", Bari (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Tursi F","lastName":"Tursi","firstName":"Francesco","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UOC Riabilitazione Specialistica Cardiorespiratoria, Codogno Hospital, ASST Lodi, Lodi (Italy)."}]}},{"fullName":"Ortolani V","lastName":"Ortolani","firstName":"Valeria","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy)."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8028-5453"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"21","journalIssueId":4296569,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Multidiscip Respir Med","title":"Multidisciplinary respiratory medicine","essn":"2049-6958","issn":"1828-695X","nlmid":"101477642","isoabbreviation":"Multidiscip Respir Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1142","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Severe asthma patients receiving biologic therapy require accurate functional assessment and longitudinal monitoring. Spirometry and impulse oscillometry (IOS) may provide complementary information on lung function. The aim of this study was to compare spirometry and IOS in severe asthma patients treated with biologics, evaluating their concordance and clinical relevance, and to assess longitudinal changes in asthma control, oral corticosteroid (OCS) use, exacerbations, and healthcare utilization over 18 months.<h4>Methods</h4>In this real-world dual-centre longitudinal study, 40 patients with severe asthma treated with biologic therapy underwent spirometry and IOS at baseline and after 6, 12, and 18 months. Functional findings were compared between the two techniques and correlated with asthma control, OCS use, exacerbations, hospitalizations, and healthcare utilization.<h4>Results</h4>Significant clinical improvement was observed over 18 months, with reductions in exacerbations, hospitalizations, healthcare utilization and OCS use, alongside improved ACT scores. FEF25-75% showed partial functional improvement and significant correlations with IOS indices, particularly R5-R20 (r=-0.52 to -0.65) and AX (r=-0.30 to -0.54), supporting the complementary role of oscillometry in longitudinal assessment.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Biologic therapy significantly reduced the clinical burden of severe asthma. IOS may provide complementary information alongside spirometry for longitudinal functional monitoring in real-life setting.","affiliation":"Allergy and Clinical Immunology Unit, L. Sacco Hospital, Milan (Italy).","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Spirometry","Severe asthma","Biological Therapy","Real-world Study","Impulse Oscillometry Lung Function"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.5826/mrm.2026.1142"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13722450"},{"availabilit